{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TFrTgD1boe6orhUX32gmMY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n2\t\n3\tStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n4\t§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\n5\tdesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n6\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\n7\ton branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\n8\tdesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\n9\tdraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\n10\tthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n11\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n12\t`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\n13\tapplies the codex review\n14\t`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n15\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n16\t(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\n17\tcorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\n18\trecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\n19\tvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n20\t(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\n21\ta specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n22\t6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\n23\texhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\n24\treview\n25\t`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n26\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\n27\tslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\n28\tmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\n29\twith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\n30\troot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\n31\tand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\n32\tcross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\n33\tand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). **Revision 4\n34\t(2026-10-13, Architect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice\n35\t6b merged (`1db08f7d`: `c3d37586` plus the fixes `a396f996`, `f650e046`,\n36\t`bafdec74`), so that §§2.5-2.6, 5.2, 6.1, 6.3, 7, 8.2 state what\n37\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (`lift-task`, `lower-task`,\n38\t`check-stores!`, `check-pendings!`, `check-dependencies!`,\n39\t`check-custody!`, `discharge!`, `live-pcs`) does; every amended clause\n40\tis marked \"**Amended after 6b**\" with what changed and why, §10.6\n41\tinventories them, and §11 lists the design choices the code made that\n42\tthe owner has not ruled on. Sources: the engineer report\n43\t`collab/1792270000000-engineer-6b-task-transport.report.md` (design bugs\n44\tB1-B4, deviations 1-10) and the three codex reviews\n45\t(`collab/1792280000000-…`, `1792300000000-…`, `1792320000000-…`). The\n46\tfrozen document is not changed by this one.\n47\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\n48\tunitarity invariant, and to\n49\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n50\t(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n51\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\n52\tand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n53\t\n54\tEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\n55\ttext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\n56\tdocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n57\t`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\n58\tcited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\n59\tits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n60\t(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\n61\tpayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\n62\tto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n63\t\n64\tContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\n65\tframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\n66\tthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\n67\tlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\n68\tverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\n69\tquestions · §12 portability.\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n74\t\n75\t### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n76\t\n77\t1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n78\t   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n79\t   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n80\t2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n81\t   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n82\t3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n83\t   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n84\t   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n85\t   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n86\t   tree set** is incomplete.\n87\t4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n88\t   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n89\t5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n90\t6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n91\t   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n92\t   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n93\t   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n94\t7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n95\t   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n96\t   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n97\t\n98\t### 0.2 The answer\n99\t\n100\tA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\n101\tmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\n102\tframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n103\t(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\n104\tenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\n105\tA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\n106\tevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\n107\tprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\n108\twindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n109\t`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n110\t`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n111\t`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\n112\tvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\n113\tcode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\n114\tnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\n115\tseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\n116\tregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\n117\tdestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\n118\tis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\n119\ta kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\n120\tthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\n121\tre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\n122\tfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\n123\tframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\n124\tand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n125\t(needs 5a/5b).\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## 1. The canonical frame\n130\t\n131\t### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n132\t\n133\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n134\t`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n135\t`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\n136\tand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n137\t\n138\t```clojure\n139\tact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\n140\ttail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n141\tret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\t- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n145\t  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n146\t  §1.5).\n147\t- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n148\t  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n149\t  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n150\t- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n151\t  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n152\t  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n153\t- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n154\t  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n155\t  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n156\t  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n157\t  §1.6).\n158\t- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n159\t  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n160\t- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n161\t  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n162\t  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n163\t  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n164\t- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n165\t  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n166\t  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n167\t  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n168\t  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n169\t\n170\t### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n171\t\n172\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n173\t\n174\t```clojure\n175\t;; act state\n176\t{:yin.k/state :act\n177\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n178\t :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n179\t :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n180\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n181\t :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n182\t            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n183\t :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n184\t\n185\t;; tail state\n186\t{:yin.k/state :tail\n187\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n188\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n189\t :yin.k/k [ … ]\n190\t :yin.k/pending {…}}\n191\t```\n192\t\n193\tThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\n194\tof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\n195\tharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\n196\ttwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n197\t\n198\t---\n199\t\n200\t## 2. The row carrier\n201\t\n202\tThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n203\t**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\n204\treaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\n205\tspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\n206\tpermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n207\t`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n208\t`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\n209\tsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n210\t\n211\t### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n212\t\n213\t**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\n214\tVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\n215\tgate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\n216\tone. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\n217\tleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\n218\tsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\n219\tThe wire unit is the draft's:\n220\t\n221\t```clojure\n222\t{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n223\t```\n224\t\n225\t**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n226\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n227\t**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\n228\tengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\n229\treceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n230\t(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n231\t\n232\t### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n233\t\n234\t**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\n235\tcontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\n236\tdigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n237\t`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n238\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\n239\tthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\n240\tare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\n241\tcell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n242\t\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\n243\thandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\n244\tbytes of the key as spelled.\n245\t\n246\t### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n247\t\n248\t**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\n249\tand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n250\t\n251\t| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n252\t|---|---|---|\n253\t| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n254\t| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n255\t| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n256\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n257\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n258\t| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n259\t| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n260\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n261\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n262\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n263\t\n264\t`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\n265\tstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\n266\tmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\n267\tredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n268\t`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n269\t\n270\t### 2.4 Pending rows\n271\t\n272\t**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n273\t`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n274\t:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\n275\tchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\n276\tpending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\n277\tthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\n278\tmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n279\t`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n280\t(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n281\t\n282\t### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n283\t\n284\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n285\t\n286\t- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n287\t  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n288\t  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n289\t  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n290\t- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n291\t  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n292\t  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n293\t  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n294\t  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n295\t  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n296\t  amendment §7.3, kept).\n297\t- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n298\t  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n299\t  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n300\t- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n301\t  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n302\t\n303\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), what travels and what does not**\n304\t(engineer deviations 4, 6, 7; codex round 1 accepted all three):\n305\t\n306\t- **The store slice is the whole root store.** `lift-task` walks every\n307\t  entry of `(:store m)` (`store-node`), and every parked record travels\n308\t  (`parked-table` over all of `(:parked m)`), each judged by P4. No\n309\t  reachability pruning of the store is performed. *Why:* the kernel\n310\t  holds no liveness information for store entries; the conservative\n311\t  slice is correct and the draft's \"exactly the reachable records\" was\n312\t  never implemented.\n313\t- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n314\t  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n315\t  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n316\t  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n317\t  :missing-module-store`.\n318\t- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n319\t  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n320\t  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n321\t  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n322\t  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n323\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n324\t  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n325\t- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.** The lift emits `custody nil`\n326\t  (fork), and the lower refuses any non-nil custody as `:yin.k/unsupported\n327\t  :custody`; the exclusive and holder path is phase 7 (§8.4).\n328\t\n329\t### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n330\t\n331\t**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n332\t\n333\t```clojure\n334\t[T :task\n335\t   version          ; 3\n336\t   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n337\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n338\t   role             ; :root | :child\n339\t   id-counter       ; N\n340\t   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n341\t   store-cref\n342\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n343\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n344\t   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n345\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n346\t   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n347\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n348\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n349\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n350\t   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n351\t   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n352\t```\n353\t\n354\tNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\n355\tprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\n356\tstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n357\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** of that table only the fork rows are\n358\treachable in phase 6; the lift writes `custody nil` and the lower's step\n359\t10 (`check-custody!`) refuses any non-nil value `:yin.k/unsupported\n360\t:custody` (§2.5, §7.2). *Why:* the exclusive and holder path is phase\n361\t7's; carrying a custody arm no reader admits would be an untested slot.\n362\t\n363\t### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n364\t\n365\t**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\n366\tare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n367\t`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\n368\tkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n369\t`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n370\t`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\n371\tvalidator decides.\n372\t\n373\t---\n374\t\n375\t## 3. The frame validator\n376\t\n377\t### 3.1 The pure input contract\n378\t\n379\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\n380\tbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\n381\treturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n382\t(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n383\t\n384\t```clojure\n385\t(frame/defect frame code context task)\n386\t;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n387\t;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n388\t;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n389\t;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n390\t;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n391\t;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n392\t;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n393\t;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n394\t\n395\t(frame/task-defect task)\n396\t;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n397\t;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n398\t```\n399\t\n400\tNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n401\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n402\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n403\t(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n404\t(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\n405\tdecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\n406\tthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\n407\tkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\n408\tsame function on the same inputs.\n409\t\n410\t### 3.2 The rules\n411\t\n412\tRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\n413\tcarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\n414\tbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n415\t(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n416\t(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n417\t(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\n418\tbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n419\t(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n420\t\n421\t**Structure (every frame).**\n422\t\n423\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n424\t|---|---|---|\n425\t| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n426\t| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n427\t| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n428\t| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n429\t\n430\t**Code (every frame).**\n431\t\n432\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n433\t|---|---|---|\n434\t| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n435\t| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n436\t| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n437\t\n438\t**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n439\t\n440\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n441\t|---|---|---|\n442\t| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n443\t| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n444\t| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n445\t| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n446\t| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n447\t| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n448\t\n449\t**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n450\t\n451\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n452\t|---|---|---|\n453\t| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n454\t\n455\t**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n456\t\n457\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n458\t|---|---|---|\n459\t| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n460\t| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n461\t| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n462\t\n463\t**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n464\t\n465\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n466\t|---|---|---|\n467\t| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n468\t| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n469\t\n470\t**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n471\t\n472\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n473\t|---|---|---|\n474\t| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n475\t\n476\t**Pending and contexts.**\n477\t\n478\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n479\t|---|---|---|\n480\t| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n481\t| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n482\t| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n483\t| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n484\t\n485\tRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\n486\tcannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\n487\tenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\n488\tevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\n489\tstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n490\t\n491\t### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n492\t\n493\t**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\n494\treview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\n495\thashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\n496\tpending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\n497\tcomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\n498\tcontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n499\t`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\n500\tjudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\n501\tis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\n502\tfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n503\t(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\n504\tand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\n505\tover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\n506\tevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n507\t`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\n508\treconstructs.\n509\t\n510\t---\n511\t\n512\t## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n513\t\n514\t### 4.1 What a layout is\n515\t\n516\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\n517\tstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\n518\tfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\n519\tphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\n520\tframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\n521\tadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\n522\tcanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\n523\tBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n524\t§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n525\t\n526\t**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n527\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n528\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n529\t`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\n530\tthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n531\t`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n532\t`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n533\t`link-format`).\n534\t\n535\t### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n536\t\n537\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n538\t(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n539\t`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n540\t:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n541\t`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n542\t`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n543\t\n544\t**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\n545\tv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n546\t`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\n547\tslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\n548\tthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\n549\tis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n550\t`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\n551\tsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\n552\tboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\n553\twith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\n554\tcover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n555\t(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\n556\toperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\n557\tslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\n558\tinverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n559\t`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\n560\tcanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n561\t\n562\t**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n563\t:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n564\t:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n565\t\n566\t1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n567\t   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n568\t   checksum).\n569\t2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n570\t   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n571\t   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n572\t3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n573\t   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n574\t4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n575\t   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n576\t   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n577\t   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n578\t   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n579\t   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n580\t5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n581\t6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n582\t   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n583\t   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n584\t   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n585\t   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n586\t   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n587\t   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n588\t   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n589\t7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n590\t   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n591\t\n592\t**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n593\t(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n594\t(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\n595\tin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n596\t`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\n597\ttail state carries no window and no `E`).\n598\t\n599\t**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\n600\timage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\n601\tvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n602\t`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n603\t`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\n604\trecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n605\t`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\n606\tfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\n607\trow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n608\t… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n609\t`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\n610\taggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n611\t(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n612\t\n613\t**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n614\t`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n615\t`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n616\t(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n617\t`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\n618\tthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\n619\tthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n620\t`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\n621\tdelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n622\t\n623\t### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n624\t\n625\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\n626\tand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\n627\tpops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n628\t`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n629\t(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\n630\tthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\n631\tcontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\n632\tthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n633\t\n634\t**Static half.**\n635\t\n636\t1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n637\t   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n638\t   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n639\t   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n640\t   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n641\t   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n642\t   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n643\t2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n644\t   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n645\t   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n646\t   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n647\t   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n648\t   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n649\t   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n650\t   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n651\t   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n652\t   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n653\t   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n654\t   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n655\t   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n656\t   syntax).\n657\t\n658\t**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n659\t`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\n660\toperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\n661\tcaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\n662\tslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n663\t\n664\t```\n665\tslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\n666\tslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\n667\tslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n668\t```\n669\t\n670\t(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n671\t0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n672\t\n673\t**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n674\t:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\n675\ta tail `:call`:\n676\t\n677\t1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n678\t   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n679\t2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n680\t   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n681\t3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n682\t4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n683\t   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n684\t   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n685\t   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n686\t   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n687\t   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n688\t5. pending from the engine fields.\n689\t\n690\t**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n691\t`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n692\t`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\n693\tlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n694\t\n695\t**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n696\t`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\n697\tconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n698\t`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n699\t(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n700\t`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n701\t:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\n702\tthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\n703\tb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n704\t`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\n705\tresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n706\t(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n707\t(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\n708\tbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\n709\tand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\n710\tstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\n711\tthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\n712\tposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\n713\tresult.\n714\t\n715\t**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\n716\tresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n717\t(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n718\t`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n719\t(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\n720\tenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\n721\tthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\n722\tdelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\n723\tlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n724\t(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\n725\tits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\n726\ttail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\n727\texisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\n728\tdelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\n729\ttruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n730\t`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\n731\tof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n732\t(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\n733\ttheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\n734\tLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n735\t`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n736\t:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n737\t:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n738\t**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\n739\tframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\n740\tcontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n741\t(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\n742\titem 8). File:\n743\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\n744\tregister kernel needs no change.\n745\t\n746\t### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n747\t\n748\t**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\n749\tphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\n750\tnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n751\t`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\n752\tlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\n753\tone per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n754\t\n755\t**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n756\t`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n757\t\n758\t```\n759\tE = (merge free-env\n760\t           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n761\t           …\n762\t           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n763\t    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n764\t```\n765\t\n766\tThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n767\t(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n768\t(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\n769\titself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\n770\tduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n771\t`engine/bind-params`'s.\n772\t\n773\t**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\n774\tcounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n775\t\n776\t```clojure\n777\t(let [f (fn [] x)]\n778\t  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n779\t```\n780\t\n781\tAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\n782\tearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\n783\tthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\n784\tclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\n785\ttask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\n786\tcarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\n787\tchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n788\t\n789\t```\n790\tfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\n791\tF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n792\t```\n793\t\n794\t`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\n795\tframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\n796\tunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n797\t`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\n798\tnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\n799\tvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\n800\tframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\n801\tharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\n802\tevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\n803\tdesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n804\t`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n805\t\n806\t**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\n807\tentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\n808\tclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n809\t§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\n810\tbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\n811\ttask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n812\t`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n813\t\n814\t### 4.5 What a layout never does\n815\t\n816\t**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\n817\tcontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\n818\tstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n819\t§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\n820\tinvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\n821\tinstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n822\t\n823\t---\n824\t\n825\t## 5. The safepoint harness\n826\t\n827\t### 5.1 What it proves"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc5fbdae-afe7-4b77-8da9-cb26d4729e39","uuid":"28eb3e9f-fba8-4202-96bb-e552a49e8c38","timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:19:10.149Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-6c/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n\nStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\ndesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\non branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\ndesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\ndraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\nthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\napplies the codex review\n`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\ncorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\nrecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\nvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\na specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\nexhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\nreview\n`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\nslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\nmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\nwith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\nroot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\nand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\ncross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\nand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). **Revision 4\n(2026-10-13, Architect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice\n6b merged (`1db08f7d`: `c3d37586` plus the fixes `a396f996`, `f650e046`,\n`bafdec74`), so that §§2.5-2.6, 5.2, 6.1, 6.3, 7, 8.2 state what\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (`lift-task`, `lower-task`,\n`check-stores!`, `check-pendings!`, `check-dependencies!`,\n`check-custody!`, `discharge!`, `live-pcs`) does; every amended clause\nis marked \"**Amended after 6b**\" with what changed and why, §10.6\ninventories them, and §11 lists the design choices the code made that\nthe owner has not ruled on. Sources: the engineer report\n`collab/1792270000000-engineer-6b-task-transport.report.md` (design bugs\nB1-B4, deviations 1-10) and the three codex reviews\n(`collab/1792280000000-…`, `1792300000000-…`, `1792320000000-…`). The\nfrozen document is not changed by this one.\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\nunitarity invariant, and to\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\nand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n\nEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\ntext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\ndocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\ncited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\nits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\npayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\nto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n\nContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\nframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\nthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\nlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\nverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\nquestions · §12 portability.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n\n### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n\n1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n   tree set** is incomplete.\n4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n\n### 0.2 The answer\n\nA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\nmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\nframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\nenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\nA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\nevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\nprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\nwindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\nvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\ncode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\nnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\nseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\nregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\ndestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\nis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\na kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\nthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\nre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\nfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\nframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\nand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n(needs 5a/5b).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The canonical frame\n\n### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\nand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n\n```clojure\nact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\ntail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\nret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n```\n\n- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n  §1.5).\n- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n  §1.6).\n- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n\n### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n\n```clojure\n;; act state\n{:yin.k/state :act\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n\n;; tail state\n{:yin.k/state :tail\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n :yin.k/k [ … ]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}\n```\n\nThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\nof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\nharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\ntwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The row carrier\n\nThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\nreaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\nspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\npermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\nsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n\n### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n\n**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\nVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\ngate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\none. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\nleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\nsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\nThe wire unit is the draft's:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n```\n\n**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\nengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\nreceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n\n### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\ncontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\ndigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\nthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\nare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\ncell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\nhandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\nbytes of the key as spelled.\n\n### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\nand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n\n| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n\n`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\nstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\nmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\nredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n\n### 2.4 Pending rows\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\nchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\npending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\nthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\nmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n\n### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n\n- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n  amendment §7.3, kept).\n- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), what travels and what does not**\n(engineer deviations 4, 6, 7; codex round 1 accepted all three):\n\n- **The store slice is the whole root store.** `lift-task` walks every\n  entry of `(:store m)` (`store-node`), and every parked record travels\n  (`parked-table` over all of `(:parked m)`), each judged by P4. No\n  reachability pruning of the store is performed. *Why:* the kernel\n  holds no liveness information for store entries; the conservative\n  slice is correct and the draft's \"exactly the reachable records\" was\n  never implemented.\n- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :missing-module-store`.\n- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.** The lift emits `custody nil`\n  (fork), and the lower refuses any non-nil custody as `:yin.k/unsupported\n  :custody`; the exclusive and holder path is phase 7 (§8.4).\n\n### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n\n**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N\n   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n```\n\nNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\nstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** of that table only the fork rows are\nreachable in phase 6; the lift writes `custody nil` and the lower's step\n10 (`check-custody!`) refuses any non-nil value `:yin.k/unsupported\n:custody` (§2.5, §7.2). *Why:* the exclusive and holder path is phase\n7's; carrying a custody arm no reader admits would be an untested slot.\n\n### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n\n**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\nare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\nkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\nvalidator decides.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The frame validator\n\n### 3.1 The pure input contract\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\nbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\nreturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n\n```clojure\n(frame/defect frame code context task)\n;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n\n(frame/task-defect task)\n;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n```\n\nNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\ndecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\nthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\nkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\nsame function on the same inputs.\n\n### 3.2 The rules\n\nRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\ncarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\nbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\nbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n\n**Structure (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n\n**Code (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n\n**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n\n**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n\n**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n\n**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n\n**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n\n**Pending and contexts.**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n\nRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\ncannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\nenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\nevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\nstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n\n### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n\n**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\nreview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\nhashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\npending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\ncomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\ncontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\njudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\nis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\nfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\nand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\nover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\nevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\nreconstructs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n\n### 4.1 What a layout is\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\nstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\nfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\nphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\nframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\nadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\ncanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\nBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n\n**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\nthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n`link-format`).\n\n### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n\n**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\nv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\nslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\nthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\nis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\nsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\nboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\nwith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\ncover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\noperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\nslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\ninverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\ncanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n\n1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n   checksum).\n2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\nin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\ntail state carries no window and no `E`).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\nimage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\nvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\nrecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\nfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\nrow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\naggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n\n**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\nthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\nthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\ndelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n\n### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\nand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\npops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\nthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\ncontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\nthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n\n**Static half.**\n\n1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n   syntax).\n\n**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\noperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\ncaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\nslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n\n```\nslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\nslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\nslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n```\n\n(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\na tail `:call`:\n\n1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n5. pending from the engine fields.\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\nlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\nconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\nthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\nb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\nresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\nbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\nand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\nstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\nthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\nposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\nresult.\n\n**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\nresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\nenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\nthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\ndelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\nlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\nits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\ntail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\nexisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\ndelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\ntruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\nof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\ntheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\nLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\nframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\ncontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\nitem 8). File:\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\nregister kernel needs no change.\n\n### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\nphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\nnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\nlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\none per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n\n**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n\n```\nE = (merge free-env\n           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n           …\n           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n```\n\nThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\nitself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\nduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n`engine/bind-params`'s.\n\n**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\ncounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n\n```clojure\n(let [f (fn [] x)]\n  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n```\n\nAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\nearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\nthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\nclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\ntask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\ncarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\nchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n\n```\nfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\nF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n```\n\n`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\nframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\nunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\nnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\nvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\nframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\nharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\nevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\ndesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n\n**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\nentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\nclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\nbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\ntask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n\n### 4.5 What a layout never does\n\n**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\ncontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\nstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\ninvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\ninstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The safepoint harness\n\n### 5.1 What it proves","numLines":827,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2118,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TFrTgD1boe6orhUX32gmMY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RRGdGknD9zSyaF5MPka5vf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# UCF transported state as tuples: the row carrier (body version 3)\n2\t\n3\tStatus: **Draft, proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-10 from the owner\n4\tdiscussion recorded in\n5\t`public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog`\n6\tPart Three; revised the same day after the codex Architect review\n7\t`collab/1791500000000-architect-ucf-transport-tuples-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n8\t(round 1: thirteen findings; round 2: eight; round 3: seven; all\n9\taddressed below, and nine answers adopted in §10). §10 also lists which\n10\tparts of this document are design proposals rather than consequences of\n11\tthe owner's rulings.\n12\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), to UCF §7 in\n13\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md),\n14\tand to the version-2 amendment\n15\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md),\n16\twhose body is the fact list this carrier must reproduce. Follows the row\n17\tconventions of [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md).\n18\tNothing here is implemented. This document specifies body version 3, the\n19\tone carrier; older bodies are not read.\n20\t\n21\t> **Amendment 2026-10-11.** This draft is amended by\n22\t> `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §10.3 (R1-R11, codex\n23\t> rulings of 2026-10-10) for the register-shaped canonical machine of\n24\t> `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`, and the amended text below\n25\t> **supersedes** the draft's earlier §5.2 (the `:regs`/`:stack`/\n26\t> `stack-base` frame rows), its §8 step 7 (stack-base checks) and its\n27\t> \"the `\"v3\"` stamp does not move\" positioning: the carrier is body\n28\t> version 3 with one engine-less profile\n29\t> `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`, reads no other body\n30\t> version, carries canonical vectors by A, `:yin.k/trees` and the task's\n31\t> free environment, never a native image, and its frames are `:act` and\n32\t> `:tail` rows judged by the frame validator. Amended: the status\n33\t> paragraph (R1a), §2 (R1, R7), §5.1 `:reified` (R3), §5.2 (R2), §5.4\n34\t> `:parked-table` (R4), §5.5 (R5, R10), §8 steps 1 and 7 (R6a, R6), §9\n35\t> (R8), §10.3-10.8 (R7, R8, R9), §11 (R9); §3 kept verbatim (R11).\n36\t> Completed against continuation §3.3 and §7.1 on review (codex,\n37\t> 2026-10-11): §8 step 7 runs `frame/task-defect` once per body before\n38\t> its frames; §8 step 9's census roots and §9's lift walk include the\n39\t> initial free environment; §5.2 cites rule A2.\n40\t\n41\t## 1. Stance\n42\t\n43\tThree owner rulings fix the shape of this document.\n44\t\n45\t1. *Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints.* The Universal AST is\n46\t   the canonical code; the instruction vector is its deterministic\n47\t   lowering; a safepoint is `(segment-address, pc)` into that vector\n48\t   (UCF §7.3.2, §7.4.1). This carrier changes nothing about code\n49\t   identity, safepoints, or execution profiles.\n50\t2. *The transported state is a tuple set.* Sharing and the literal/marker\n51\t   distinction stop being special cases: a reference is a row id in a\n52\t   typed position, sharing is Merkle, and a program's data is wrapped\n53\t   where it can be confused with a reference. What does not collapse is\n54\t   the per-entity decision between content and identity, which this\n55\t   carrier makes a property of the row kind and then commits to the root\n56\t   through a census.\n57\t3. *Tuples are the transport form only.* They exist between a lift and a\n58\t   lower. At rest they are rows like any other and queryable by the\n59\t   ordinary interpreter. Nothing executes them; the destination lowers\n60\t   them into its native representation.\n61\t\n62\tThis proposal chooses positional rows: a row kind fixes a slot list, one\n63\ttable serves encoder and decoder, and a row's Merkle id commits to its\n64\tchildren inline, as code rows already do. EAV is expressive enough and\n65\tnothing in the invariants rules it out; positional rows are chosen for\n66\tcompactness, closedness, and consistency with the existing row\n67\tconventions, not because EAV was rejected by review.\n68\t\n69\t## 2. Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n70\t\n71\t**Body version 3, one profile.** The carrier is body version 3 and reads\n72\tno other version (owner, 2026-10-11: no legacy): a body of version 0, 1\n73\tor 2 is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` at the gate and is never normalised or\n74\tupgraded. The profile map is `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`;\n75\tthere is no engine key, because the frame is the canonical\n76\tregister-shaped machine's and the executing kernel is the receiving\n77\tcomposition's choice: the stack and register kernels lower the same rows\n78\tthrough their layouts (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n79\t§2.1, §4). Any other profile map is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n80\t\n81\t## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n82\t\n83\tEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n84\t`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\n85\tas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n86\t\n87\t**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\n88\tsomething to run on before any row is interpreted:\n89\t\n90\t```clojure\n91\t{:yin.k/handoff true\n92\t :yin.k/version 3\n93\t :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n94\t :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\tUnlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\n98\tany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so every walk is over a map from\n99\tid to one row. The gate's walk over install children follows only\n100\t`:install` child crefs and keeps an **active recursion-path set**: a\n101\tcref that leaves the rows, or a child that is already on the active\n102\tpath, stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable`. A child reached again\n103\t*off* the active path is not an error: two install entries whose\n104\tchildren have identical content (two identical halted children, say)\n105\tshare one child row by Merkle construction, the structural graph is a\n106\tDAG, and each install entry still restores as its own native task\n107\tinstance with its own task-local cursor cells and its own contextual custody\n108\tvalidation. Completed content validation is cached per row; instance\n109\trestoration never is. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what\n110\tis hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in\n111\t`:rows` is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n112\t\n113\t**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`: **the same\n114\tcall, and therefore the same digest, as code addresses `A`**\n115\t(`ucf/code-address` is `(jing/segment-key v)`), which is Jing's default\n116\talgorithm, BLAKE3 at the time of writing (`dao.jing.cbor.md`,\n117\t`dao.jing.hash-registry.md`). The carrier mints no second addressing\n118\tscheme beside A. Uniqueness, which a canonical address scheme needs, is\n119\tsupplied by the **carrier version**, not by naming an algorithm here:\n120\t**body version 3 uses BLAKE3 permanently**; this is an immutable\n121\tmapping from carrier version to digest, never the reader's current\n122\tdefault, and a later Jing default requires another carrier version,\n123\texactly as it would for A. Version-3 verification and re-emission (§6,\n124\t§8) always use BLAKE3, whatever the reader's default is at the time;\n125\tthis matters because root body bytes contain child-row addresses, so\n126\tthe numbering procedure's leaf keys depend on the digest and are\n127\tcanonical *within* a carrier version. Addresses are self-describing\n128\t(`:segment/blake3-…`, `:segment/sha256-…`); a reader verifies each id\n129\tunder the algorithm its address names, as Jing always does, and refuses\n130\tan id whose algorithm is not the one version 3 mints under\n131\t(`:yin.k/undecodable`). Equal bodies are one row. The id is Merkle: it\n132\tcommits to every row id in the body.\n133\t\n134\t**Identity rows** are cursor cells (UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor,\n135\tcursor cell, cursor-ref, cell\", which defines all four words; this document\n136\tuses them exactly as defined there). Before 2026-10-12 this document called\n137\tthe cursor cell a \"cell\" and spelled its tags `:cells`, `:cell-body`,\n138\t`[:cell C]`, `:yin.k/c-<n>`; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps those\n139\tspellings. A cursor cell has a task-local id `C`\n140\tin the form `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n141\tnumbering (the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical walk), never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\n142\tcarries it: a cursor-cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\n143\tquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/cc-1` are unrelated.\n144\t\n145\t**The census.** A cursor cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\n146\tare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\n147\tcursor cell commits to the cursor cell's identity but not to what the cursor cell holds. The\n148\troot therefore carries a **cursor-cell census**, a content row\n149\t\n150\t```clojure\n151\t[X :cursor-cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n152\t[Bᵢ :cursor-cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n153\t```\n154\t\n155\tthat maps every cursor-cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\n156\tslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cursor cell's contents. A\n157\tchange to any cursor cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\n158\tthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\n159\tauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\n160\tlist exactly the cursor cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\n161\troot and per install child.\n162\t\n163\t**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n164\t\n165\t- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cursor-cell iref in a value, a\n166\t  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n167\t  and a `:cursor-cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n168\t  (data), so no cursor cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n169\t  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n170\t  validates, it does not trust construction.\n171\t- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n172\t  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n173\t  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n174\t  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n175\t  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n176\t  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n177\t  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n178\t  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n179\t  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n180\t  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n181\t  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n182\t\n183\t## 4. The value union\n184\t\n185\tEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\n186\ta two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n187\t\n188\t| `V` | Meaning |\n189\t|---|---|\n190\t| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n191\t| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n192\t| `[:cursor-cell C]` | a reference to the identity cursor cell `C` |\n193\t\n194\tThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\n195\tliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\n196\tcan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n197\t`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\n198\tis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n199\t\n200\t**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\n201\tarms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n202\t`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n203\t(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\n204\tRational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\n205\tsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\n206\tvalues and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\n207\tretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\n208\tare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n209\tkind `:byte-string`.\n210\t\n211\t**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\n212\tportability and whether a value reaches a reference. Reference-free\n213\tretained metadata on a *literal* travels as Jing preserves it and is\n214\thashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\n215\t**any collection that must become a row and carries retained metadata\n216\trefuses**, whether the metadata holds a reference (copying it as data\n217\twould carry source coordinates and seals unremapped) or is ordinary\n218\treference-free data such as `{:doc \"x\"}`: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n219\t`:metadata` naming the path. This is a proposed limitation of this\n220\tcarrier, not an inherited UCF requirement (§10). Metadata a reader strips\n221\tis not retained metadata and plays no part.\n222\t\n223\tA guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\n224\tdata inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n225\t\n226\tA collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\n227\treference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\n228\tholds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\n229\tand **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n230\t`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\n231\tcollections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\n232\tdecision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\n233\tcollection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n234\t\n235\t**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n236\t§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\n237\tguest literals and contain no guest values, namely install responses,\n238\tlink envelopes, operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data\n239\tslot holds plain canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that\n240\tgoverns it (an install response by the linker's delivery checks, an\n241\top-id by §7.4.3 r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these\n242\tslots `D`. A retained **FFI request envelope is not structural data**:\n243\t`dao.stream.apply/request?` permits unknown envelope keys, and its\n244\targuments are guest values. It therefore travels as the existing\n245\tencoder carries it (`handoff.cljc`): the **complete envelope,\n246\trecursively encoded as a `V`** (a `:map` row, or `[:lit m]` when it\n247\treaches no reference), so that every key is preserved, closures stay\n248\tportable, and resource references go through the census. No closed\n249\tenvelope row narrows the open request protocol.\n250\t\n251\tPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\n252\tnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\n253\ttheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n254\t\n255\t## 5. The row table\n256\t\n257\tKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\n258\tnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n259\tnaming the row and slot. **One ordering rule** governs every emitted row\n260\tand every receiver check: \"sorted\" means ordered by the Jing canonical\n261\tbytes of the sort key *as spelled in the emitted row*, cursor-cell numbers\n262\tincluded, under the final numbering of §6. The spellings (keys) of §6\n263\tare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\n264\tof an emitted row. Ordered slots are part of the schema because\n265\tthey determine the Merkle id.\n266\t\n267\t`N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\n268\tcode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cursor-cell id,\n269\t`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\n270\tonly the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\n271\tof another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n272\t`:parked-table`, `:cursor-cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\n273\thave unique keys, and `:set` rows unique elements, under canonical bytes\n274\t**and** under portable decoded equality, since Jing's own map and set\n275\trules do not apply to a key or element list spelled as a vector.\n276\t\n277\t### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n278\t\n279\t| Kind | Slots | Decodes to |\n280\t|---|---|---|\n281\t| `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n282\t| `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n283\t| `:set` | `V …` sorted (§5 preamble) | set |\n284\t| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by key | map |\n285\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n286\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n287\t| `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n288\t| `:prim` | `name-S` | the named primitive after the profile check (UCF §7.5.2); the profile lives in requires |\n289\t| `:stream` | `identity  descriptor` | a stream marker; both plain, authenticated at lift and re-sealed at lower |\n290\t| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation: the decoded act state of frozen §4.2, no pending |\n291\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | the keyed `act` state (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §1.2) reached through `P`: resolves in the task's parked table; never duplicates the state |\n292\t\n293\t`:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n294\t§6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\n295\treceiver-owned native continuation from its act; a parked reference\n296\treconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`. A reified value's act is\n297\tvalidated in context `:captured` (continuation §3.2 P3): its `pc−1` is a\n298\t`:current-continuation` and it has no pending.\n299\t\n300\t### 5.2 Frame rows: states, windows, waits, frames (content rows)\n301\t\n302\t(Replaced 2026-10-11 by `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n303\t§2.3, under frozen `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §4.2.)\n304\t\n305\t| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n306\t|---|---|---|\n307\t| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n308\t| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n309\t| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n310\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n311\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n312\t| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n313\t| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n314\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n315\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (§5.1) |\n316\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n317\t\n318\t`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\n319\tstructural rows, never a `V`; §4's \"a reference-free collection must be\n320\t`[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is redundant\n321\twith the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (continuation §3.2 rule\n322\tA2), as `pc` is redundant with the pending and checked. The frame rows\n323\tare in bijection with the keyed canonical frame of continuation §1.2\n324\t(`rows->frame`, `frame->rows`, continuation §2.7).\n325\t\n326\t### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n327\t\n328\tOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\n329\tslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\n330\tthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n331\t`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n332\t\n333\t| reason | slots after the reason |\n334\t|---|---|\n335\t| `:next` | `cursor-cell-C` |\n336\t| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n337\t| `:ffi` | `cursor-cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n338\t| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-V  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cursor-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n339\t| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cursor-cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n340\t| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cursor-cell-C` |\n341\t| `:install` | `name-S` |\n342\t\n343\tThe retained FFI request's `envelope-V` is the complete envelope map,\n344\trecursively encoded under §4's ordinary rules: `[:lit m]` when it\n345\treaches no reference (the common case, arguments such as `[1 2]`), a\n346\t`:map` row otherwise. Lower decodes it and retries it verbatim; it\n347\tnever recomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\n348\ta cursor cell's `:cursor-cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\n349\tenvelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\n350\tenvelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n351\tpresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n352\twriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n353\tand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n354\t`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\n355\twhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\n356\tretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\n357\tAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\n358\tslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n359\t`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\n360\tis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n361\t\n362\t### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n363\t\n364\t| Kind | Slots |\n365\t|---|---|\n366\t| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n367\t| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n368\t| `:parked-table` | `P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …` sorted by P (each state an `:act` or a `:tail`) |\n369\t| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n370\t| `:cursor-cells` | the census, §3 |\n371\t| `:cursor-cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n372\t\n373\tValidation of a parked state reads decoded task context, never an\n374\tengine: the table key `P` and the set of call ids the task's live\n375\t`:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pendings carry are the `task` input of\n376\t`frame/defect` (continuation §3.1), under which an `:act` at a `:park`\n377\tsite is an explicit park and an `:act` at an `:ffi-call` site or a\n378\t`:tail` at a tail call under a live call id is FFI bookkeeping.\n379\t\n380\tResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cursor cells lower into\n381\tthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\n382\tM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\n383\tsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\n384\treachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n385\t§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\n386\tcontract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\n387\tchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\n388\tmeans verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\n389\tthe response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\n390\tid equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\n391\tis delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\n392\tcontract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\n393\tamendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n394\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §5.2 B1, §7.2):** the\n395\tmerged lower (`rows/check-install!`) verifies, before any attachment:\n396\tthe phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\n397\tresponse is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` whose\n398\t`:yin.module/name` is the row's module and whose `:yin.module/exports`\n399\tis a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map whose `:value` the\n400\treceiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`); and the carried\n401\tchild task's kind is `:blocked` (`:install-child`), the only kind the\n402\tengine retains at a safepoint (a halted or parked child is linked in the\n403\tsweep it halts in, so phase `:running` is a wire phase the codec accepts\n404\tbut the engine never exposes at a safepoint). The link-id-equals-parent\n405\tand image-address-equals-manifest checks of the paragraph above are\n406\t**not** implemented (continuation §11 Q3).\n407\t\n408\t### 5.5 The root\n409\t\n410\t```clojure\n411\t[T :task\n412\t   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n413\t   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}, the one profile map (§2)\n414\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n415\t   role             ; :root | :child\n416\t   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n417\t   free-env-cref    ; a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (continuation §6.3)\n418\t   store-cref\n419\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n420\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n421\t   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n422\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (continuation §6.1)\n423\t   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n424\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n425\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n426\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n427\t   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n428\t   custody]         ; plain, see below\n429\t```\n430\t\n431\t(Root replaced 2026-10-11 by continuation §2.6: `free-env-cref` added,\n432\t`contract` is the one profile map, `code` holds `\"v4\"` canonical vectors,\n433\t`requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`.) No `layout` slot and no native image:\n434\tthose were the positional profiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n435\t\n436\tKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\n437\tparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\n438\tnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\n439\tneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n440\tnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n441\tordered waits.\n442\t\n443\t`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n444\t§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n445\t\n446\t| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n447\t|---|---|---|---|\n448\t| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n449\t| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n450\t| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n451\t| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n452\t| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n453\t\n454\tThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\n455\tempty-header fork representation: a fork root proposes nothing and is\n456\tnever upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n457\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\n458\tunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n459\t\n460\tThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n461\taddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n462\tare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n463\tplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n464\tkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n465\tthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n466\t\n467\t## 6. Canonical cursor-cell numbering\n468\t\n469\tCursor-cell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\n470\ttwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cursor cell's number must depend on\n471\teverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\n472\telsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\n473\tholding cursor cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\n474\tlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\n475\tcan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n476\ttopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n477\twhich any algorithm is checked.\n478\t\n479\t**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering is\n480\tdefined as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, a function\n481\tof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\n482\tsets, maps and the census (proof in\n483\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\n484\tminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\n485\tcannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\n486\tthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n487\t(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\n488\tinterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\n489\tnumbering assigned today.\n490\t\n491\t**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\n492\twhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n493\t`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\n494\tis pre-order in the table order of §5, so every position is ordered\n495\texcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\n496\tthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\n497\tmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\n498\talready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\n499\tcount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\n500\tmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\n501\tare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\n502\tre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\n503\tproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\n504\tunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\n505\tsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n506\t\n507\t**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\n508\tprocedure below over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is over\n509\tthe `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the output;\n510\ta **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or map from\n511\tthe enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's **key**.\n512\tA **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the synthetic\n513\tordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of members of\n514\tone container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank** is the index\n515\tof its census body label `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`\n516\tamong the census's distinct labels in bytewise order; its **occurrence\n517\tcount** is the number of positions of the task (census and install\n518\tchildren excluded) that hold it. Install children are numbered as their\n519\town tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n520\t\n521\t**The transformation and the key.** When the walk reaches a `:set` or\n522\t`:map` it replaces the container, for its own purposes, by the vector of\n523\tits members in key order. The key of a member is the **spelling** of that\n524\tmember produced by a key walk under the numbering at that moment (every\n525\tmember of one container is keyed from that one numbering):\n526\t\n527\t- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n528\t  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n529\t- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n530\t  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n531\t  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n532\t  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n533\t  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n534\t- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n535\t  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n536\t  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n537\t  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n538\t  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n539\t  group the key walk could not certify (the tie test below) is spelled in\n540\t  place as `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n541\t\n542\tKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n543\t`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n544\t`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\n545\tof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n546\t`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search.\n547\t\n548\t**The walk.**\n549\t\n550\t- *W1, order.* Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\n551\t  slots in table order (§5); ordered repeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`,\n552\t  `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows with structural keys (`:bindings`,\n553\t  `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and `module-stores`)\n554\t  in the Jing canonical byte order of the key; `:window` in ascending\n555\t  register order. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task`\n556\t  row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is entered as the transformation\n557\t  orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an entry's key\n558\t  before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the walk's queue\n559\t  (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups within one\n560\t  container in key order).\n561\t- *W2, meeting.* When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n562\t  `C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\n563\t  receives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\n564\t  is passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`;\n565\t  the census row stays the contents table.\n566\t- *W3, postponed ties.* After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\n567\t  order: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering\n568\t  and treated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\n569\t  re-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\n570\t  queue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\n571\t  remain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\n572\t  test.\n573\t\n574\t**The tie test.** A surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common\n575\tkey is complete (contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor\n576\tcells of its members are pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs\n577\tin a member of any other surviving group of the same walk. (At this point\n578\tevery unnumbered cell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii)\n579\tsay: the cells of each member are confined to that member.) If every\n580\tsurviving group passes, all of them are entered, each group's members in\n581\tthe order the host lists them (the numbering note's §2 shows the rows do\n582\tnot depend on it), and the walk returns to W3. If any group fails, the\n583\t**root walk refuses** and a **key walk stops**: its surviving groups are\n584\tspelled as `[:tie …]` residue and its key is incomplete. The test inside\n585\ta key walk reads only the member being keyed; it never reads the\n586\tenclosing state. The test is sound and cheap: (i)–(iii) are exactly what\n587\tthe proof needs for the index-matched swap of two members to be an\n588\tautomorphism of the state; whatever it refuses may still be\n589\tinterchangeable (numbering note §3 says what), but nothing it accepts can\n590\tbe host-order dependent.\n591\t\n592\t**Refusal.** The root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n593\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\n594\tholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\n595\ta member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n596\t`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\n597\tan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n598\t`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\n599\tgroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\n600\tto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\n601\tThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n602\tThe receiver's recomputation (§8 step 6) translates only this refusal,\n603\tfor the root or any install child, to `:yin.k/undecodable`\n604\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the carried root id\n605\t(a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a resource refusal\n606\tpropagates as itself. Numbering is checked after step 5 and before frame\n607\tvalidation (step 7), so a wrong numbering is reported as\n608\t`:noncanonical-cursor-cells` and an unresolved one as\n609\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` even when the task also has a frame defect.\n610\t\n611\t**Budgets and gates.**\n612\t\n613\t- `:cursor-cells`: the census's pair count is charged first, before the\n614\t  census map, ranks, counts or any walk is built.\n615\t- `:depth`: enforced second, **before any recursive traversal of the\n616\t  task**, by one bounded iterative scan (an explicit stack, never the\n617\t  host stack) over rows, members, structural and literal data and the\n618\t  census, install children excluded (each is gated by its own run). The\n619\t  depth of a position is its slot steps from the task (a member step\n620\t  counts one, a map entry's key or value two) plus, inside data, its\n621\t  collection nesting; a position deeper than `:depth` refuses\n622\t  `[:depth]`. After the scan every recursive read of the task (cell\n623\t  scans, occurrence counts, memo-key hashing, spelling, encoding) nests\n624\t  at most `:depth` levels, so no input can overflow the host stack\n625\t  before the gate fires. The walk checks its own depth as well. Default\n626\t  256.\n627\t- `:work`: one unit per position scanned (the depth scan), visited\n628\t  (walk, key walk, cell scans, count and rank preparation), keyed (one\n629\t  per member per keying, so the number of key walks and memo lookups is\n630\t  charged) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key walk's memo key, and,\n631\t  **before every CBOR encoding made for a comparison** (keys, rank\n632\t  labels, structural keys), the representation bound of the value\n633\t  encoded: 128 per node or scalar, 4 per character, 16 plus the length\n634\t  per byte payload, an upper bound of the encoding's bytes. The\n635\t  estimator is iterative and budget-aware: it runs against the budget\n636\t  remaining at the call and stops as soon as its running total exceeds\n637\t  it, so it never reads to the end of a value it cannot afford.\n638\t  Spellings hold input literals by reference, so their own size is their\n639\t  spelled positions. Every comparison encoding is thus charged before it\n640\t  is made, by a conservative abstract bound of its encoded bytes and of\n641\t  the encoder's intermediate nodes; the budget bounds allocation up to a\n642\t  constant factor of the host's representation, not byte for byte, and\n643\t  the input itself is not counted. No polynomial bound is claimed; the\n644\t  budget is the bound. Default `4194304`. A literal of more than about\n645\t  30000 scalars inside a guest set or map exceeds it; in an ordered\n646\t  position a literal is never spelled or encoded and costs one unit per\n647\t  position of its data in the depth scan.\n648\t- `:rows`, `:bytes`: the decoder's, unchanged.\n649\t\n650\tExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of §10.10\n651\t(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\n652\tverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n653\t\n654\t**Totality.** The root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before\n655\temission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection\n656\tfrom the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell\n657\tno walk reaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status`\n658\tcarrying the missing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a\n659\tpartially numbered carrier.\n660\t\n661\t**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\n662\ta source-cursor-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\n663\tnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\n664\tprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\n665\tcarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n666\t`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\n667\tcommitment is thereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance\n668\tdecision are invariant under the host's enumeration order of every set,\n669\tmap and census (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host\n670\torder, entering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable,\n671\tcannot change the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\n672\tenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\n673\tkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\n674\tprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\n675\tany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\n676\tnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\n677\trefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\n678\t\n679\tUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n680\tdifferent states sharing a closure over the same cursor cell may number it\n681\tdifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\n682\tcross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\n683\tnever affected rows below a cursor cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\n684\tprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cursor-cell C]`\n685\tmaps to that entry; two references to one cursor cell share it and one\n686\treference per cursor cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n687\t\n688\t## 7. Equality and the content address\n689\t\n690\tThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n691\tthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cursor cell\n692\tid through the references, and to every cursor cell's contents through the\n693\tcensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\n694\tand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\n695\trenaming of cursor-cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\n696\tthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\n697\tthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\n698\tJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\n699\tsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cursor cells. Jing is\n700\tasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n701\t\n702\tOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\n703\tthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\n704\toccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\n705\toccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n706\t\n707\t## 8. Validation on lower\n708\t\n709\tValidation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\n710\tone attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n711\t\n712\t1. **Gate.** Decode the wire map's canonical bytes retaining numeric\n713\t   classes; the tag is present and the outer version is the exact\n714\t   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\n715\t   set). Reject duplicate row ids and a `:yin.k/root` that names no\n716\t   `:task` row (`:yin.k/undecodable`). Walk install children from the\n717\t   root in canonical module-name order with an **active recursion-path\n718\t   set** (§3), following only `:install` child crefs; a cref outside the\n719\t   rows, a child already on the active path, or a non-`:task` child\n720\t   stops the gate as `:yin.k/undecodable`; a child reached again off\n721\t   the path is gated once and its result cached. Gate every\n722\t   child's version, then every profile map against the one profile map\n723\t   of §2, then require all versions 3\n724\t   and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n725\t   `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n726\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n727\t2. **Structure, non-recursive.** Every row's kind is tabled with the\n728\t   right arity; every structural slot holds its tabled kind by local\n729\t   inspection; every `V` has one of the three tags; every `D` is in the\n730\t   canonical bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules\n731\t   of §5.5 hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy\n732\t   table. Nothing in this step follows a reference.\n733\t3. **Graph.** Every cref and every `[:row B]` names a present row of a\n734\t   kind admissible in that position; the structural edge family is\n735\t   acyclic (checked with an active-path walk, bounded by the row count);\n736\t   every row is reachable from the root through structural edges (an\n737\t   unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cursor-cell C]` names a\n738\t   census entry and every census entry is reached **through the task's\n739\t   own roots, the census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and\n740\t   per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked\n741\t   table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every\n742\t   `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child is a\n743\t   `:child` task. After this step every traversal below is finite and\n744\t   safe.\n745\t4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals `(jing/segment-key body)`\n746\t   under the algorithm version 3 mints with, and its address names that\n747\t   algorithm; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n748\t   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n749\t   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n750\t   code key.\n751\t5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\n752\t   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\n753\t   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\n754\t   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\n755\t   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\n756\t   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\n757\t   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\n758\t   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\n759\t   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\n760\t   lose a binding on lower.\n761\t6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\n762\t   root and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n763\t   `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the\n764\t   tie refusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\n765\t   recomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\n766\t   by the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\n767\t   is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\n768\t7. **Frames.** For each body (the root and every install child), first\n769\t   decode its task context (parked key, live FFI call ids, the root's\n770\t   free environment from `free-env-cref`) and judge it once by\n771\t   `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame/task-defect` (continuation §3.2 rule\n772\t   X1: the free environment is a sorted, unique symbol-keyed map with no\n773\t   reserved name); this runs for every body, a `:halted` body with no\n774\t   frames included, since no frame rule establishes X1. Then every\n775\t   `:act`, `:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is\n776\t   decoded to its keyed frame and judged by\n777\t   `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame/defect` with its context (`:wait`,\n778\t   `:captured`, `:parked`) and that task context; a defect of either\n779\t   function is `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path\n780\t   (continuation §3.3). This step runs after canonical numbering (step 6)\n781\t   and before pending validation (step 8).\n782\t8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n783\t   agreement of §5.3 (cursor-cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n784\t   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n785\t   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n786\t9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n787\t   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, the initial free\n788\t   environment `free-env`, store, module stores, result, children; the\n789\t   free environment is a root because a value reachable only through it\n790\t   can carry closures, streams and primitives, continuation §7.1) and\n791\t   require the carried `requires` and `code` map to agree with it\n792\t   exactly: a declared segment not reached or a reached segment not\n793\t   declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`, and every reached segment has a\n794\t   non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry (UCF §7.6.1a). Then\n795\t   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\n796\t   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\n797\t   with cursor cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\n798\t   complete for every `store-of` reached.\n799\t10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n800\t    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n801\t    never adds missing header keys.\n802\t\n803\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §7.2).** The merged\n804\t`rows/lower-task` runs steps 8-10 as four stages, **each over the whole\n805\ttask tree before the next, and all before the first attachment** (step\n806\t11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n807\t(`:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`); (b) pendings and install\n808\trecords: role, every `:install` pending names a carried install, kind\n809\tconsistency, parked id, every install record as §5.4 as amended\n810\t(`:role :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :install-phase\n811\t:install-response :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n812\t`:yin.k/segments` equals `code`'s key set (`:requires`), every segment\n813\thas a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry, every `store-of` of this task's\n814\tclosures and environments names one of **this task's** module-store\n815\trows (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores …}`), every\n816\tvector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`:segment`), every\n817\tclosure row names a closure tuple of its vector with the same binders\n818\t(`:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`), every `:prim` is the receiver's\n819\t(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: nil only (`:yin.k/unsupported\n820\t:custody`). Of step 8's text, the op-id rules and the correlation\n821\tagreement are not implemented (fork-only custody has no `next-op-seq`;\n822\tcontinuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\n823\trows (continuation §11 Q2), and `requires` beyond segments, trees and\n824\tprimitives (modules, streams, cursor profiles) is not evaluated on\n825\tlower; free names are discharged at lift only (continuation §7.1).\n826\tRefusals are thrown `ex-info` with `:yin.k/status` and a `:yin.k/kind`.\n827\t11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n828\t    the receiving composition.\n829\t12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n830\t    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n831\t    private resource entry per cursor cell, the parked table, children first\n832\t    (one native task instance **per install entry**, even where two\n833\t    entries share a child row), then waits in order; adopt `max(local,\n834\t    carried)` for the id counter; publish the machine only after every\n835\t    child succeeds; run, poll, mint, append, close or initialize nothing\n836\t    during lower.\n837\t\n838\tDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n839\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n840\t`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n841\t(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`,\n842\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n843\t`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n844\tthe three kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`,\n845\t`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\n846\t\n847\t## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n848\t\n849\t**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cursor cells (§6);\n850\twalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the initial free\n851\tenvironment (continuation §6.3, §7.1), the result and each install\n852\tchild, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n853\t§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation\n854\t§7.1 B4):** the merged order is quiescence and safepoint, kind, free\n855\tenvironment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\n856\tstores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\n857\tcoverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\n858\tthe assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\n859\twhole root store and every parked record; custody is nil. The nested carrier's value table\n860\t(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\n861\ttable becomes the census.\n862\t\n863\t**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n864\t\n865\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n866\t\n867\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n868\t  version-3 state `S` under the one profile of §2 **for which `lower(S)`\n869\t  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n870\t  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n871\t  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n872\t  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n873\t  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n874\t  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n875\t  record, not minted; cursor-cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n876\t  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n877\t  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n878\t  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n879\t- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n880\t  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n881\t  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n882\t  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n883\t- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n884\t  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n885\t  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n886\t  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n887\t  reference machine yields.\n888\t\n889\tThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. The Stage D/E corpus,\n890\tre-encoded as version-3 rows, is the oracle for the first law.\n891\t\n892\t## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n893\t\n894\tAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\n895\tmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n896\t\n897\t1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n898\t   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n899\t   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n900\t   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n901\t   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n902\t   for the census.\n903\t2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n904\t   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n905\t   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cursor-cell ids\n906\t   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n907\t   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n908\t   layout.\n909\t3. **The nested grammar.** Rows are the carrier authority.\n910\t4. **Contract stamp.** The code contract carried is `\"v4\"`, the\n911\t   register-shaped machine's; the carrier version is 3; the two are\n912\t   independent, and neither names a legacy value.\n913\t5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:act` rows is acceptable;\n914\t   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n915\t   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n916\t   context.\n917\t6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before an implementation\n918\t   brief claims execution conformance for an engine **outside the three\n919\t   repository lanes** (the semantic-register kernel and the stack and\n920\t   register kernels, whose reconstruction is the layouts of\n921\t   `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §4 and needs no value\n922\t   profile), and not drafted here: admitted values, numeric behaviour,\n923\t   key equality, metadata, resource and profile requirements, refusal\n924\t   sets, and safepoint reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness\n925\t   alone does not establish execution parity; version 3 carries the\n926\t   canonical frame; a stack or register kernel reconstructs it through\n927\t   its layout (continuation §4), which is heterogeneous reconstruction,\n928\t   not translation between native formats; foreign-engine *value*\n929\t   profiles for engines outside this repository remain a separate\n930\t   document.\n931\t\n932\tAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted (7 and 8 are\n933\tdeleted 2026-10-11: there are no other profile tables and no legacy\n934\treader):\n935\t\n936\t9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n937\t   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cursor-cell <class>]`,\n938\t   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n939\t   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of the\n940\t   discovery-order procedure, which is invariant under renaming and host\n941\t   enumeration.\n942\t\n943\t10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n944\t    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n945\t    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n946\t    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth and\n947\t    traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions\n948\t    and representation bytes, charged before any spelling is encoded),\n949\t    with elapsed time as an optional\n950\t    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n951\t    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n952\t    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n953\t    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never\n954\t    substitutes host enumeration order for the canonical walk, and never\n955\t    accepts an unfinished check. The\n956\t    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n957\t    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n958\t\n959\t**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\n960\tdocument makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\n961\trulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\n962\tname them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\n963\tanother carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\n964\tdefined by discovery order, and the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal;\n965\tthe wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\n966\texplicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; one engine-less profile\n967\t(continuation §2.1); the explicit root `free-env` (continuation §6.3);\n968\tthe byte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; binding the row digest to\n969\tthe carrier version rather than to a named algorithm (§3; the owner\n970\truled 2026-10-10 that rows use the same digest as code addresses, which\n971\tis Jing's default, rather than a separate SHA-256 pin); and positional\n972\trows over EAV (§1). Preserving the complete FFI\n973\tenvelope (§5.3) is *not* on this list: it is the inherited behaviour,\n974\tand narrowing it would have been the new protocol decision.\n975\t\n976\tStill open after this revision: none beyond the work-bound limits\n977\t(§10.10).\n978\t\n979\t## 11. What this does not change\n980\t\n981\tCode identity (A), safepoints,\n982\tpending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n983\tthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n984\tprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n985\tcustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n986\toutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (three lift kinds and three\n987\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n988\tversion for the same facts. The\n989\tArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n990\tviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n991\tcontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\n992\tthey are.\n993\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc5fbdae-afe7-4b77-8da9-cb26d4729e39","uuid":"45cc52a1-fa08-405c-b595-2cd20b5cdfc1","timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:19:10.660Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-6c/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md","content":"# UCF transported state as tuples: the row carrier (body version 3)\n\nStatus: **Draft, proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-10 from the owner\ndiscussion recorded in\n`public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog`\nPart Three; revised the same day after the codex Architect review\n`collab/1791500000000-architect-ucf-transport-tuples-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(round 1: thirteen findings; round 2: eight; round 3: seven; all\naddressed below, and nine answers adopted in §10). §10 also lists which\nparts of this document are design proposals rather than consequences of\nthe owner's rulings.\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), to UCF §7 in\n[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md),\nand to the version-2 amendment\n[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md),\nwhose body is the fact list this carrier must reproduce. Follows the row\nconventions of [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md).\nNothing here is implemented. This document specifies body version 3, the\none carrier; older bodies are not read.\n\n> **Amendment 2026-10-11.** This draft is amended by\n> `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §10.3 (R1-R11, codex\n> rulings of 2026-10-10) for the register-shaped canonical machine of\n> `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`, and the amended text below\n> **supersedes** the draft's earlier §5.2 (the `:regs`/`:stack`/\n> `stack-base` frame rows), its §8 step 7 (stack-base checks) and its\n> \"the `\"v3\"` stamp does not move\" positioning: the carrier is body\n> version 3 with one engine-less profile\n> `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`, reads no other body\n> version, carries canonical vectors by A, `:yin.k/trees` and the task's\n> free environment, never a native image, and its frames are `:act` and\n> `:tail` rows judged by the frame validator. Amended: the status\n> paragraph (R1a), §2 (R1, R7), §5.1 `:reified` (R3), §5.2 (R2), §5.4\n> `:parked-table` (R4), §5.5 (R5, R10), §8 steps 1 and 7 (R6a, R6), §9\n> (R8), §10.3-10.8 (R7, R8, R9), §11 (R9); §3 kept verbatim (R11).\n> Completed against continuation §3.3 and §7.1 on review (codex,\n> 2026-10-11): §8 step 7 runs `frame/task-defect` once per body before\n> its frames; §8 step 9's census roots and §9's lift walk include the\n> initial free environment; §5.2 cites rule A2.\n\n## 1. Stance\n\nThree owner rulings fix the shape of this document.\n\n1. *Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints.* The Universal AST is\n   the canonical code; the instruction vector is its deterministic\n   lowering; a safepoint is `(segment-address, pc)` into that vector\n   (UCF §7.3.2, §7.4.1). This carrier changes nothing about code\n   identity, safepoints, or execution profiles.\n2. *The transported state is a tuple set.* Sharing and the literal/marker\n   distinction stop being special cases: a reference is a row id in a\n   typed position, sharing is Merkle, and a program's data is wrapped\n   where it can be confused with a reference. What does not collapse is\n   the per-entity decision between content and identity, which this\n   carrier makes a property of the row kind and then commits to the root\n   through a census.\n3. *Tuples are the transport form only.* They exist between a lift and a\n   lower. At rest they are rows like any other and queryable by the\n   ordinary interpreter. Nothing executes them; the destination lowers\n   them into its native representation.\n\nThis proposal chooses positional rows: a row kind fixes a slot list, one\ntable serves encoder and decoder, and a row's Merkle id commits to its\nchildren inline, as code rows already do. EAV is expressive enough and\nnothing in the invariants rules it out; positional rows are chosen for\ncompactness, closedness, and consistency with the existing row\nconventions, not because EAV was rejected by review.\n\n## 2. Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n\n**Body version 3, one profile.** The carrier is body version 3 and reads\nno other version (owner, 2026-10-11: no legacy): a body of version 0, 1\nor 2 is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` at the gate and is never normalised or\nupgraded. The profile map is `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`;\nthere is no engine key, because the frame is the canonical\nregister-shaped machine's and the executing kernel is the receiving\ncomposition's choice: the stack and register kernels lower the same rows\nthrough their layouts (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n§2.1, §4). Any other profile map is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n\n## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n\nEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\nas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n\n**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\nsomething to run on before any row is interpreted:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true\n :yin.k/version 3\n :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n```\n\nUnlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\nany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so every walk is over a map from\nid to one row. The gate's walk over install children follows only\n`:install` child crefs and keeps an **active recursion-path set**: a\ncref that leaves the rows, or a child that is already on the active\npath, stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable`. A child reached again\n*off* the active path is not an error: two install entries whose\nchildren have identical content (two identical halted children, say)\nshare one child row by Merkle construction, the structural graph is a\nDAG, and each install entry still restores as its own native task\ninstance with its own task-local cursor cells and its own contextual custody\nvalidation. Completed content validation is cached per row; instance\nrestoration never is. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what\nis hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in\n`:rows` is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`: **the same\ncall, and therefore the same digest, as code addresses `A`**\n(`ucf/code-address` is `(jing/segment-key v)`), which is Jing's default\nalgorithm, BLAKE3 at the time of writing (`dao.jing.cbor.md`,\n`dao.jing.hash-registry.md`). The carrier mints no second addressing\nscheme beside A. Uniqueness, which a canonical address scheme needs, is\nsupplied by the **carrier version**, not by naming an algorithm here:\n**body version 3 uses BLAKE3 permanently**; this is an immutable\nmapping from carrier version to digest, never the reader's current\ndefault, and a later Jing default requires another carrier version,\nexactly as it would for A. Version-3 verification and re-emission (§6,\n§8) always use BLAKE3, whatever the reader's default is at the time;\nthis matters because root body bytes contain child-row addresses, so\nthe numbering procedure's leaf keys depend on the digest and are\ncanonical *within* a carrier version. Addresses are self-describing\n(`:segment/blake3-…`, `:segment/sha256-…`); a reader verifies each id\nunder the algorithm its address names, as Jing always does, and refuses\nan id whose algorithm is not the one version 3 mints under\n(`:yin.k/undecodable`). Equal bodies are one row. The id is Merkle: it\ncommits to every row id in the body.\n\n**Identity rows** are cursor cells (UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor,\ncursor cell, cursor-ref, cell\", which defines all four words; this document\nuses them exactly as defined there). Before 2026-10-12 this document called\nthe cursor cell a \"cell\" and spelled its tags `:cells`, `:cell-body`,\n`[:cell C]`, `:yin.k/c-<n>`; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps those\nspellings. A cursor cell has a task-local id `C`\nin the form `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\nnumbering (the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical walk), never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\ncarries it: a cursor-cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\nquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/cc-1` are unrelated.\n\n**The census.** A cursor cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\nare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\ncursor cell commits to the cursor cell's identity but not to what the cursor cell holds. The\nroot therefore carries a **cursor-cell census**, a content row\n\n```clojure\n[X :cursor-cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n[Bᵢ :cursor-cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n```\n\nthat maps every cursor-cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\nslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cursor cell's contents. A\nchange to any cursor cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\nthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\nauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\nlist exactly the cursor cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\nroot and per install child.\n\n**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n\n- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cursor-cell iref in a value, a\n  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n  and a `:cursor-cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n  (data), so no cursor cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n  validates, it does not trust construction.\n- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n\n## 4. The value union\n\nEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\na two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n\n| `V` | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n| `[:cursor-cell C]` | a reference to the identity cursor cell `C` |\n\nThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\nliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\ncan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\nis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n\n**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\narms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\nRational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\nsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\nvalues and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\nretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\nare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\nkind `:byte-string`.\n\n**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\nportability and whether a value reaches a reference. Reference-free\nretained metadata on a *literal* travels as Jing preserves it and is\nhashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\n**any collection that must become a row and carries retained metadata\nrefuses**, whether the metadata holds a reference (copying it as data\nwould carry source coordinates and seals unremapped) or is ordinary\nreference-free data such as `{:doc \"x\"}`: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:metadata` naming the path. This is a proposed limitation of this\ncarrier, not an inherited UCF requirement (§10). Metadata a reader strips\nis not retained metadata and plays no part.\n\nA guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\ndata inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n\nA collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\nreference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\nholds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\nand **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\ncollections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\ndecision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\ncollection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n\n**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\nguest literals and contain no guest values, namely install responses,\nlink envelopes, operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data\nslot holds plain canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that\ngoverns it (an install response by the linker's delivery checks, an\nop-id by §7.4.3 r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these\nslots `D`. A retained **FFI request envelope is not structural data**:\n`dao.stream.apply/request?` permits unknown envelope keys, and its\narguments are guest values. It therefore travels as the existing\nencoder carries it (`handoff.cljc`): the **complete envelope,\nrecursively encoded as a `V`** (a `:map` row, or `[:lit m]` when it\nreaches no reference), so that every key is preserved, closures stay\nportable, and resource references go through the census. No closed\nenvelope row narrows the open request protocol.\n\nPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\nnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\ntheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n\n## 5. The row table\n\nKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\nnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\nnaming the row and slot. **One ordering rule** governs every emitted row\nand every receiver check: \"sorted\" means ordered by the Jing canonical\nbytes of the sort key *as spelled in the emitted row*, cursor-cell numbers\nincluded, under the final numbering of §6. The spellings (keys) of §6\nare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\nof an emitted row. Ordered slots are part of the schema because\nthey determine the Merkle id.\n\n`N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\ncode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cursor-cell id,\n`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\nonly the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\nof another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n`:parked-table`, `:cursor-cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\nhave unique keys, and `:set` rows unique elements, under canonical bytes\n**and** under portable decoded equality, since Jing's own map and set\nrules do not apply to a key or element list spelled as a vector.\n\n### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots | Decodes to |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n| `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n| `:set` | `V …` sorted (§5 preamble) | set |\n| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by key | map |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n| `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n| `:prim` | `name-S` | the named primitive after the profile check (UCF §7.5.2); the profile lives in requires |\n| `:stream` | `identity  descriptor` | a stream marker; both plain, authenticated at lift and re-sealed at lower |\n| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation: the decoded act state of frozen §4.2, no pending |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | the keyed `act` state (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §1.2) reached through `P`: resolves in the task's parked table; never duplicates the state |\n\n`:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n§6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\nreceiver-owned native continuation from its act; a parked reference\nreconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`. A reified value's act is\nvalidated in context `:captured` (continuation §3.2 P3): its `pc−1` is a\n`:current-continuation` and it has no pending.\n\n### 5.2 Frame rows: states, windows, waits, frames (content rows)\n\n(Replaced 2026-10-11 by `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n§2.3, under frozen `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §4.2.)\n\n| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (§5.1) |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n\n`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\nstructural rows, never a `V`; §4's \"a reference-free collection must be\n`[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is redundant\nwith the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (continuation §3.2 rule\nA2), as `pc` is redundant with the pending and checked. The frame rows\nare in bijection with the keyed canonical frame of continuation §1.2\n(`rows->frame`, `frame->rows`, continuation §2.7).\n\n### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n\nOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\nslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\nthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n| reason | slots after the reason |\n|---|---|\n| `:next` | `cursor-cell-C` |\n| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n| `:ffi` | `cursor-cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-V  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cursor-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cursor-cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cursor-cell-C` |\n| `:install` | `name-S` |\n\nThe retained FFI request's `envelope-V` is the complete envelope map,\nrecursively encoded under §4's ordinary rules: `[:lit m]` when it\nreaches no reference (the common case, arguments such as `[1 2]`), a\n`:map` row otherwise. Lower decodes it and retries it verbatim; it\nnever recomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\na cursor cell's `:cursor-cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\nenvelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\nenvelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\npresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\nwriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\nand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\nwhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\nretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\nAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\nslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\nis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n| `:parked-table` | `P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …` sorted by P (each state an `:act` or a `:tail`) |\n| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n| `:cursor-cells` | the census, §3 |\n| `:cursor-cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n\nValidation of a parked state reads decoded task context, never an\nengine: the table key `P` and the set of call ids the task's live\n`:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pendings carry are the `task` input of\n`frame/defect` (continuation §3.1), under which an `:act` at a `:park`\nsite is an explicit park and an `:act` at an `:ffi-call` site or a\n`:tail` at a tail call under a live call id is FFI bookkeeping.\n\nResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cursor cells lower into\nthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\nM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\nsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\nreachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\ncontract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\nchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\nmeans verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\nthe response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\nid equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\nis delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\ncontract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\namendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §5.2 B1, §7.2):** the\nmerged lower (`rows/check-install!`) verifies, before any attachment:\nthe phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\nresponse is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` whose\n`:yin.module/name` is the row's module and whose `:yin.module/exports`\nis a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map whose `:value` the\nreceiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`); and the carried\nchild task's kind is `:blocked` (`:install-child`), the only kind the\nengine retains at a safepoint (a halted or parked child is linked in the\nsweep it halts in, so phase `:running` is a wire phase the codec accepts\nbut the engine never exposes at a safepoint). The link-id-equals-parent\nand image-address-equals-manifest checks of the paragraph above are\n**not** implemented (continuation §11 Q3).\n\n### 5.5 The root\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}, the one profile map (§2)\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   free-env-cref    ; a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (continuation §6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (continuation §6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, see below\n```\n\n(Root replaced 2026-10-11 by continuation §2.6: `free-env-cref` added,\n`contract` is the one profile map, `code` holds `\"v4\"` canonical vectors,\n`requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`.) No `layout` slot and no native image:\nthose were the positional profiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n\nKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\nparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\nnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\nneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\nnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\nordered waits.\n\n`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n\n| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n\nThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\nempty-header fork representation: a fork root proposes nothing and is\nnever upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\nunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n\nThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\naddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\nare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\nplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\nkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\nthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n\n## 6. Canonical cursor-cell numbering\n\nCursor-cell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\ntwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cursor cell's number must depend on\neverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\nelsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\nholding cursor cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\nlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\ncan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\ntopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\nwhich any algorithm is checked.\n\n**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering is\ndefined as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, a function\nof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\nsets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\ncannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\nthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\ninterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\nnumbering assigned today.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the table order of §5, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure below over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is over\nthe `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the output;\na **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or map from\nthe enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's **key**.\nA **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the synthetic\nordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of members of\none container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank** is the index\nof its census body label `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`\namong the census's distinct labels in bytewise order; its **occurrence\ncount** is the number of positions of the task (census and install\nchildren excluded) that hold it. Install children are numbered as their\nown tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n**The transformation and the key.** When the walk reaches a `:set` or\n`:map` it replaces the container, for its own purposes, by the vector of\nits members in key order. The key of a member is the **spelling** of that\nmember produced by a key walk under the numbering at that moment (every\nmember of one container is keyed from that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (the tie test below) is spelled in\n  place as `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\nof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search.\n\n**The walk.**\n\n- *W1, order.* Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\n  slots in table order (§5); ordered repeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`,\n  `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows with structural keys (`:bindings`,\n  `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and `module-stores`)\n  in the Jing canonical byte order of the key; `:window` in ascending\n  register order. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task`\n  row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is entered as the transformation\n  orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an entry's key\n  before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the walk's queue\n  (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups within one\n  container in key order).\n- *W2, meeting.* When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n  `C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\n  receives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\n  is passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`;\n  the census row stays the contents table.\n- *W3, postponed ties.* After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\n  order: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering\n  and treated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\n  re-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\n  queue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\n  remain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\n  test.\n\n**The tie test.** A surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common\nkey is complete (contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor\ncells of its members are pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs\nin a member of any other surviving group of the same walk. (At this point\nevery unnumbered cell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii)\nsay: the cells of each member are confined to that member.) If every\nsurviving group passes, all of them are entered, each group's members in\nthe order the host lists them (the numbering note's §2 shows the rows do\nnot depend on it), and the walk returns to W3. If any group fails, the\n**root walk refuses** and a **key walk stops**: its surviving groups are\nspelled as `[:tie …]` residue and its key is incomplete. The test inside\na key walk reads only the member being keyed; it never reads the\nenclosing state. The test is sound and cheap: (i)–(iii) are exactly what\nthe proof needs for the index-matched swap of two members to be an\nautomorphism of the state; whatever it refuses may still be\ninterchangeable (numbering note §3 says what), but nothing it accepts can\nbe host-order dependent.\n\n**Refusal.** The root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\nThe receiver's recomputation (§8 step 6) translates only this refusal,\nfor the root or any install child, to `:yin.k/undecodable`\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the carried root id\n(a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a resource refusal\npropagates as itself. Numbering is checked after step 5 and before frame\nvalidation (step 7), so a wrong numbering is reported as\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells` and an unresolved one as\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` even when the task also has a frame defect.\n\n**Budgets and gates.**\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census's pair count is charged first, before the\n  census map, ranks, counts or any walk is built.\n- `:depth`: enforced second, **before any recursive traversal of the\n  task**, by one bounded iterative scan (an explicit stack, never the\n  host stack) over rows, members, structural and literal data and the\n  census, install children excluded (each is gated by its own run). The\n  depth of a position is its slot steps from the task (a member step\n  counts one, a map entry's key or value two) plus, inside data, its\n  collection nesting; a position deeper than `:depth` refuses\n  `[:depth]`. After the scan every recursive read of the task (cell\n  scans, occurrence counts, memo-key hashing, spelling, encoding) nests\n  at most `:depth` levels, so no input can overflow the host stack\n  before the gate fires. The walk checks its own depth as well. Default\n  256.\n- `:work`: one unit per position scanned (the depth scan), visited\n  (walk, key walk, cell scans, count and rank preparation), keyed (one\n  per member per keying, so the number of key walks and memo lookups is\n  charged) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key walk's memo key, and,\n  **before every CBOR encoding made for a comparison** (keys, rank\n  labels, structural keys), the representation bound of the value\n  encoded: 128 per node or scalar, 4 per character, 16 plus the length\n  per byte payload, an upper bound of the encoding's bytes. The\n  estimator is iterative and budget-aware: it runs against the budget\n  remaining at the call and stops as soon as its running total exceeds\n  it, so it never reads to the end of a value it cannot afford.\n  Spellings hold input literals by reference, so their own size is their\n  spelled positions. Every comparison encoding is thus charged before it\n  is made, by a conservative abstract bound of its encoded bytes and of\n  the encoder's intermediate nodes; the budget bounds allocation up to a\n  constant factor of the host's representation, not byte for byte, and\n  the input itself is not counted. No polynomial bound is claimed; the\n  budget is the bound. Default `4194304`. A literal of more than about\n  30000 scalars inside a guest set or map exceeds it; in an ordered\n  position a literal is never spelled or encoded and costs one unit per\n  position of its data in the depth scan.\n- `:rows`, `:bytes`: the decoder's, unchanged.\n\nExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of §10.10\n(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\nverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n\n**Totality.** The root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before\nemission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection\nfrom the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell\nno walk reaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status`\ncarrying the missing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a\npartially numbered carrier.\n\n**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\na source-cursor-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\nnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\nprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\ncarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\ncommitment is thereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance\ndecision are invariant under the host's enumeration order of every set,\nmap and census (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host\norder, entering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable,\ncannot change the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\nenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\nrefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\n\nUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\ndifferent states sharing a closure over the same cursor cell may number it\ndifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\ncross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\nnever affected rows below a cursor cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\nprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cursor-cell C]`\nmaps to that entry; two references to one cursor cell share it and one\nreference per cursor cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n\n## 7. Equality and the content address\n\nThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\nthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cursor cell\nid through the references, and to every cursor cell's contents through the\ncensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\nand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\nrenaming of cursor-cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\nthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\nthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\nJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\nsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cursor cells. Jing is\nasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n\nOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\nthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\noccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\noccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n\n## 8. Validation on lower\n\nValidation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\none attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n\n1. **Gate.** Decode the wire map's canonical bytes retaining numeric\n   classes; the tag is present and the outer version is the exact\n   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\n   set). Reject duplicate row ids and a `:yin.k/root` that names no\n   `:task` row (`:yin.k/undecodable`). Walk install children from the\n   root in canonical module-name order with an **active recursion-path\n   set** (§3), following only `:install` child crefs; a cref outside the\n   rows, a child already on the active path, or a non-`:task` child\n   stops the gate as `:yin.k/undecodable`; a child reached again off\n   the path is gated once and its result cached. Gate every\n   child's version, then every profile map against the one profile map\n   of §2, then require all versions 3\n   and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n   `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n2. **Structure, non-recursive.** Every row's kind is tabled with the\n   right arity; every structural slot holds its tabled kind by local\n   inspection; every `V` has one of the three tags; every `D` is in the\n   canonical bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules\n   of §5.5 hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy\n   table. Nothing in this step follows a reference.\n3. **Graph.** Every cref and every `[:row B]` names a present row of a\n   kind admissible in that position; the structural edge family is\n   acyclic (checked with an active-path walk, bounded by the row count);\n   every row is reachable from the root through structural edges (an\n   unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cursor-cell C]` names a\n   census entry and every census entry is reached **through the task's\n   own roots, the census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and\n   per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked\n   table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every\n   `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child is a\n   `:child` task. After this step every traversal below is finite and\n   safe.\n4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals `(jing/segment-key body)`\n   under the algorithm version 3 mints with, and its address names that\n   algorithm; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n   code key.\n5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\n   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\n   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\n   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\n   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\n   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\n   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\n   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\n   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\n   lose a binding on lower.\n6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\n   root and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n   `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the\n   tie refusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\n   recomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\n   by the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\n   is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\n7. **Frames.** For each body (the root and every install child), first\n   decode its task context (parked key, live FFI call ids, the root's\n   free environment from `free-env-cref`) and judge it once by\n   `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame/task-defect` (continuation §3.2 rule\n   X1: the free environment is a sorted, unique symbol-keyed map with no\n   reserved name); this runs for every body, a `:halted` body with no\n   frames included, since no frame rule establishes X1. Then every\n   `:act`, `:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is\n   decoded to its keyed frame and judged by\n   `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame/defect` with its context (`:wait`,\n   `:captured`, `:parked`) and that task context; a defect of either\n   function is `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path\n   (continuation §3.3). This step runs after canonical numbering (step 6)\n   and before pending validation (step 8).\n8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n   agreement of §5.3 (cursor-cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, the initial free\n   environment `free-env`, store, module stores, result, children; the\n   free environment is a root because a value reachable only through it\n   can carry closures, streams and primitives, continuation §7.1) and\n   require the carried `requires` and `code` map to agree with it\n   exactly: a declared segment not reached or a reached segment not\n   declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`, and every reached segment has a\n   non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry (UCF §7.6.1a). Then\n   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\n   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\n   with cursor cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\n   complete for every `store-of` reached.\n10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n    never adds missing header keys.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §7.2).** The merged\n`rows/lower-task` runs steps 8-10 as four stages, **each over the whole\ntask tree before the next, and all before the first attachment** (step\n11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`); (b) pendings and install\nrecords: role, every `:install` pending names a carried install, kind\nconsistency, parked id, every install record as §5.4 as amended\n(`:role :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :install-phase\n:install-response :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n`:yin.k/segments` equals `code`'s key set (`:requires`), every segment\nhas a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry, every `store-of` of this task's\nclosures and environments names one of **this task's** module-store\nrows (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores …}`), every\nvector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`:segment`), every\nclosure row names a closure tuple of its vector with the same binders\n(`:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`), every `:prim` is the receiver's\n(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: nil only (`:yin.k/unsupported\n:custody`). Of step 8's text, the op-id rules and the correlation\nagreement are not implemented (fork-only custody has no `next-op-seq`;\ncontinuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\nrows (continuation §11 Q2), and `requires` beyond segments, trees and\nprimitives (modules, streams, cursor profiles) is not evaluated on\nlower; free names are discharged at lift only (continuation §7.1).\nRefusals are thrown `ex-info` with `:yin.k/status` and a `:yin.k/kind`.\n11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n    the receiving composition.\n12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n    private resource entry per cursor cell, the parked table, children first\n    (one native task instance **per install entry**, even where two\n    entries share a child row), then waits in order; adopt `max(local,\n    carried)` for the id counter; publish the machine only after every\n    child succeeds; run, poll, mint, append, close or initialize nothing\n    during lower.\n\nDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`,\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\nthe three kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`,\n`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\n\n## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n\n**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cursor cells (§6);\nwalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the initial free\nenvironment (continuation §6.3, §7.1), the result and each install\nchild, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation\n§7.1 B4):** the merged order is quiescence and safepoint, kind, free\nenvironment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\nstores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\ncoverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\nthe assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\nwhole root store and every parked record; custody is nil. The nested carrier's value table\n(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\ntable becomes the census.\n\n**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n\nThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n\n- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n  version-3 state `S` under the one profile of §2 **for which `lower(S)`\n  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n  record, not minted; cursor-cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n  reference machine yields.\n\nThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. The Stage D/E corpus,\nre-encoded as version-3 rows, is the oracle for the first law.\n\n## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n\nAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\nmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n\n1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n   for the census.\n2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cursor-cell ids\n   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n   layout.\n3. **The nested grammar.** Rows are the carrier authority.\n4. **Contract stamp.** The code contract carried is `\"v4\"`, the\n   register-shaped machine's; the carrier version is 3; the two are\n   independent, and neither names a legacy value.\n5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:act` rows is acceptable;\n   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n   context.\n6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before an implementation\n   brief claims execution conformance for an engine **outside the three\n   repository lanes** (the semantic-register kernel and the stack and\n   register kernels, whose reconstruction is the layouts of\n   `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §4 and needs no value\n   profile), and not drafted here: admitted values, numeric behaviour,\n   key equality, metadata, resource and profile requirements, refusal\n   sets, and safepoint reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness\n   alone does not establish execution parity; version 3 carries the\n   canonical frame; a stack or register kernel reconstructs it through\n   its layout (continuation §4), which is heterogeneous reconstruction,\n   not translation between native formats; foreign-engine *value*\n   profiles for engines outside this repository remain a separate\n   document.\n\nAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted (7 and 8 are\ndeleted 2026-10-11: there are no other profile tables and no legacy\nreader):\n\n9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cursor-cell <class>]`,\n   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of the\n   discovery-order procedure, which is invariant under renaming and host\n   enumeration.\n\n10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth and\n    traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions\n    and representation bytes, charged before any spelling is encoded),\n    with elapsed time as an optional\n    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never\n    substitutes host enumeration order for the canonical walk, and never\n    accepts an unfinished check. The\n    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n\n**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\ndocument makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\nrulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\nname them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\nanother carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\ndefined by discovery order, and the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal;\nthe wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\nexplicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; one engine-less profile\n(continuation §2.1); the explicit root `free-env` (continuation §6.3);\nthe byte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; binding the row digest to\nthe carrier version rather than to a named algorithm (§3; the owner\nruled 2026-10-10 that rows use the same digest as code addresses, which\nis Jing's default, rather than a separate SHA-256 pin); and positional\nrows over EAV (§1). Preserving the complete FFI\nenvelope (§5.3) is *not* on this list: it is the inherited behaviour,\nand narrowing it would have been the new protocol decision.\n\nStill open after this revision: none beyond the work-bound limits\n(§10.10).\n\n## 11. What this does not change\n\nCode identity (A), safepoints,\npending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\nthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\nprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\ncustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\noutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (three lift kinds and three\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\nversion for the same facts. The\nArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\nviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\ncontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\nthey are.\n","numLines":993,"startLine":1,"totalLines":993}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RRGdGknD9zSyaF5MPka5vf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cfxxk5ES5zwnKgxLoRh2Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GYawGaTm8UzcmnFjL1t9KB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git show srvm-phase6c:src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc","description":"Show layout.cljc on the 6c branch"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1102,"cache_read_input_tokens":29978,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1102},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc5fbdae-afe7-4b77-8da9-cb26d4729e39","uuid":"68464361-20c9-4032-9cdd-3abd79b6d428","timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:19:10.689Z","request_id":"req_011Cfxxk4x4LXDqCwNgdB3kF","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GYawGaTm8UzcmnFjL1t9KB":{"command":"cd /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-6c && git show srvm-phase6c:src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc","description":"Show layout.cljc on the 6c branch"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01GYawGaTm8UzcmnFjL1t9KB":{"cwd":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-6c"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GYawGaTm8UzcmnFjL1t9KB","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (37.7KB). Full output saved to: /Users/sto/.claude/projects/-Users-sto-workspace-datomworld-docs-6c/fc5fbdae-afe7-4b77-8da9-cb26d4729e39/tool-results/bns9eze6n.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n(ns yin.vm.semantic-register.layout\n  \"Phase 6c (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md §4):\n   the de Bruijn register and stack kernels reconstruct the canonical\n   frame of the register machine through their layouts, and lower it\n   back.\n\n   Static half, per segment A: `segment` derives R(A) and H(A) and reads\n   off the register pairing (`phys`, the restricted inverse `virt`, the\n   kernel's `live` as live-out with tail boundaries `[]`), the stack\n   correspondence (`h->a`, `a->h`, every `:push` removed) and the operand\n   layout `stack-layout` (the values pushed and not yet consumed while\n   control is inside a pc's expression). A `space` indexes segments by\n   the kernels' image identities, R and H, which are checksums: A is the\n   only code identity.\n\n   Dynamic half: `lift-state` reads a kernel's wait entry, parked record\n   or reified payload as the canonical `act` or `tail` payload the\n   semantic-register evaluator mints (`{:segment A :pc :env :window :k\n   :deliver}`, `{:segment A :site :k :deliver}`, engine fields carried);\n   `lower-state` is its inverse. The register kernel's return\n   destinations are recovered from A, never from the physical `:dest`;\n   the stack kernel's operand stack is sliced `[previous-base, base)` per\n   frame and lowered with cumulative bases. `E` is the free environment\n   merged under the chain's positional frames (§4.4); the inverse reads\n   each frame back from `E` by the chain, never the free environment.\n\n   Task level: `canonical-task` presents a quiescent kernel as a\n   semantic-register machine for `yin.vm.ucf.rows/lift-task`;\n   `kernel-task` restores a machine `rows/lower-task` produced into a\n   fresh kernel; `lift-task` and `lower-task` compose them with the row\n   carrier, the lower refusing what a kernel cannot hold before any\n   stream is attached. A layout never runs code, never fills a binding\n   from the receiver and never invents a safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc5fbdae-afe7-4b77-8da9-cb26d4729e39","uuid":"ae8baa49-a5e6-4bcd-a77b-8079d0695acd","timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:19:11.847Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"(ns yin.vm.semantic-register.layout\n  \"Phase 6c (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md §4):\n   the de Bruijn register and stack kernels reconstruct the canonical\n   frame of the register machine through their layouts, and lower it\n   back.\n\n   Static half, per segment A: `segment` derives R(A) and H(A) and reads\n   off the register pairing (`phys`, the restricted inverse `virt`, the\n   kernel's `live` as live-out with tail boundaries `[]`), the stack\n   correspondence (`h->a`, `a->h`, every `:push` removed) and the operand\n   layout `stack-layout` (the values pushed and not yet consumed while\n   control is inside a pc's expression). A `space` indexes segments by\n   the kernels' image identities, R and H, which are checksums: A is the\n   only code identity.\n\n   Dynamic half: `lift-state` reads a kernel's wait entry, parked record\n   or reified payload as the canonical `act` or `tail` payload the\n   semantic-register evaluator mints (`{:segment A :pc :env :window :k\n   :deliver}`, `{:segment A :site :k :deliver}`, engine fields carried);\n   `lower-state` is its inverse. The register kernel's return\n   destinations are recovered from A, never from the physical `:dest`;\n   the stack kernel's operand stack is sliced `[previous-base, base)` per\n   frame and lowered with cumulative bases. `E` is the free environment\n   merged under the chain's positional frames (§4.4); the inverse reads\n   each frame back from `E` by the chain, never the free environment.\n\n   Task level: `canonical-task` presents a quiescent kernel as a\n   semantic-register machine for `yin.vm.ucf.rows/lift-task`;\n   `kernel-task` restores a machine `rows/lower-task` produced into a\n   fresh kernel; `lift-task` and `lower-task` compose them with the row\n   carrier, the lower refusing what a kernel cannot hold before any\n   stream is attached. A layout never runs code, never fills a binding\n   from the receiver and never invents a safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as register]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as stack]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis :as analysis]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.derive :as derive]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n\n\n(defn- refuse!\n  [status kind data]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" (merge data (assoc {} :yin.k/status status :yin.k/kind kind)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Static half: one segment\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- operands\n  \"The register operands of an A tuple, destination first, then its uses\n   in table order.\"\n  [t]\n  (into (if-some [r (code/rd t)] [r] []) (code/uses t)))\n\n\n(defn- register-operands\n  \"The `:reg`/`:regs` operands of an R tuple in table order (the `:live`\n   slot is `:data`, never paired).\"\n  [t]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [[[_ kind] x]] (case kind :reg [x] :regs x [])))\n        (map vector (get rcode/opcode-table (nth t 0)) (rest t))))\n\n\n(defn register-pairs\n  \"`[pc virtual physical]` for every register operand of every pc: A's\n   tuple and R's name their registers in the same slots (phase-5 §2.4).\"\n  [v image]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [pc]\n                  (map (fn [x s] [pc x s])\n                       (operands (nth v pc))\n                       (register-operands (nth (:instructions image) pc)))))\n        (range (count v))))\n\n\n(defn- phys-maps\n  \"Per body, `{virtual physical}` read off the pairs; the first pc that\n   names a virtual wins (register-pairing checks they agree).\"\n  [bodies pairs]\n  (reduce (fn [acc [pc x s]]\n            (let [b (code/body-index bodies pc)]\n              (if (contains? (nth acc b) x) acc (assoc-in acc [b x] s))))\n          (vec (repeat (count bodies) {}))\n          pairs))\n\n\n(defn correspondence\n  \"§4.3 static item 1: H with every `:push` removed corresponds one to one,\n   in order, with A. Answers `{:a->h [h-pc …] :h->a {h-pc a-pc}}`.\"\n  [h]\n  (reduce (fn [acc hpc]\n            (if (= :push (nth (nth h hpc) 0))\n              acc\n              (-> acc\n                  (update :a->h conj hpc)\n                  (assoc-in [:h->a hpc] (count (:a->h acc))))))\n          {:a->h [] :h->a {}}\n          (range (count h))))\n\n\n(def ^:private pushing-ops\n  \"The parse nodes whose children before the current one are pushed:\n   `:call` (the operator and earlier operands), `:ffi-call` (earlier\n   operands) and `:stream-put` (the target, while the value runs).\"\n  #{:call :ffi-call :stream-put})\n\n\n(defn- stack-layouts\n  \"§4.3 static item 2 for every pc of every body: `{pc [virtual …]}`,\n   bottom to top, over the parse tree's ancestors outermost first.\"\n  [v bodies]\n  (letfn [(walk\n            [out node pending]\n            (let [out (assoc out (:pc node) pending)\n                  pushes? (contains? pushing-ops (:op node))\n                  uses (when pushes? (code/uses (nth v (:pc node))))]\n              (reduce (fn [out [i child]]\n                        (walk out child (if pushes? (into pending (take i uses)) pending)))\n                      out\n                      (map-indexed vector (:children node)))))]\n    (reduce (fn [out body] (walk out (:expr body) [])) {} bodies)))\n\n\n(defn segment\n  \"The static facts of one admitted A vector `v`, for both kernels.\"\n  [v]\n  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n        r (derive/register-image v)\n        h (:image (derive/stack-image v))\n        pairs (register-pairs v r)]\n    (merge {:vector v\n            :address (jing/segment-key v)\n            :bodies bodies\n            :analysis (analysis/analyze v)\n            :register r\n            :register-id (rcode/register-hash r)\n            :stack h\n            :stack-id (dcode/image-hash h)\n            :pairs pairs\n            :phys (phys-maps bodies pairs)\n            :layouts (stack-layouts v bodies)}\n           (correspondence h))))\n\n\n(defn space\n  \"Segments by A, and A by each kernel's image identity. `segments` is a\n   seq of `segment` results or of A vectors.\"\n  [segments]\n  (reduce (fn [acc s]\n            (let [s (if (map? s) s (segment s))\n                  a (:address s)]\n              (-> acc\n                  (assoc-in [:segments a] s)\n                  (update-in [:register (:register-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a)\n                  (update-in [:stack (:stack-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a))))\n          {:segments {} :register {} :stack {}}\n          segments))\n\n\n(defn segment-of\n  \"The segment A names in `space`.\"\n  [space a]\n  (or (get-in space [:segments a])\n      (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n\n\n(defn- image-segment\n  \"The segment whose `kind` image is `ident`: an identity is a checksum,\n   so two A deriving one image are ambiguous and refused.\"\n  [space kind ident]\n  (let [as (get-in space [kind ident])]\n    (cond\n      (empty? as) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      (< 1 (count as)) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      :else (segment-of space (first as)))))\n\n\n(defn- inst\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:vector seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn- body-of\n  [seg pc]\n  (code/body-index (:bodies seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn phys\n  \"`phys(body(pc), x)`: the physical register R names for virtual `x` in\n   the body holding `pc`.\"\n  [seg pc x]\n  (let [s (get (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg pc)) x)]\n    (when (nil? s) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n    s))\n\n\n(defn live-out\n  \"`live-out*(p)`: the union of `L(q)` over p's actual successors (empty\n   for a tail call).\"\n  [seg p]\n  (reduce (fn [acc q] (into acc (analysis/live (:analysis seg) q)))\n          #{}\n          (analysis/successors (:vector seg) p)))\n\n\n(defn register-live\n  \"§5.6 item 3: the `live` operand R must carry at boundary `p`,\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`, the destination removed as\n   a virtual id before mapping; `[]` at a tail call.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (vec (sort (map #(phys seg p %) (disj (live-out seg p) (code/rd (inst seg p)))))))\n\n\n(defn virt\n  \"The restricted inverse of `phys` at boundary `p`: the virtual of\n   `live-out*(p)` held in physical `s`. A slot outside that image (only a\n   tampered entry names one) reads as the one virtual of the body R maps\n   there, so the frame validator judges the window; an ambiguous slot is\n   refused.\"\n  [seg p s]\n  (let [m (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg p))\n        live (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (live-out seg p)))\n        named (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (keys m)))]\n    (cond\n      (= 1 (count live)) (first live)\n      (and (empty? live) (= 1 (count named))) (first named)\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}))))\n\n\n(defn stack-layout\n  \"§4.3: the virtual registers pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside the expression at A pc `p`, bottom to top.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (get (:layouts seg) p))\n\n\n(defn chain\n  \"The closure pcs owning the body that holds `pc`, outermost first.\"\n  [seg pc]\n  (loop [b (body-of seg pc) out ()]\n    (if-let [owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) b))]\n      (recur (body-of seg owner) (conj out owner))\n      (vec out))))\n\n\n(defn- params\n  [seg c]\n  (nth (inst seg c) 2))\n\n\n(defn env-of\n  \"§4.4 forward: `E` at `pc` from the positional frames `F` (outermost\n   first, values already canonical), the task's free environment and the\n   activation's module store.\"\n  [seg pc free-env frames store-of]\n  (let [cs (chain seg pc)]\n    (when-not (= (count cs) (count frames))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :frame-depth {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/depth (count frames)}))\n    (cond-> (reduce (fn [e [c f]] (merge e (engine/bind-params (params seg c) f)))\n                    (or free-env {})\n                    (map vector cs frames))\n      (some? store-of) (assoc engine/store-of-key store-of))))\n\n\n(defn frames-of\n  \"§4.4 inverse: `F[i] = (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))` over the chain at\n   `pc`. An outer value a shadowing inner binder hides reads as the inner\n   one; the canonical frame never held it.\"\n  [seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [c] (mapv #(get env %) (params seg c))) (chain seg pc)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Kernel dispatch\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn kernel-kind\n  \"`:stack` or `:register`, by the kernel's link format.\"\n  [k]\n  (let [f (when (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel k) (:format (module/link-format k)))]\n    (cond\n      (= stack/format-tag f) :stack\n      (= register/format-tag f) :register\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :kernel {:yin.k/format f}))))\n\n\n(defn- image-pc\n  \"`[segment rel-pc]` of absolute kernel pc `pc`.\"\n  [kind k space pc]\n  (let [[ident rel] (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/image-pc k pc) (register/image-pc k pc))\n                        (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/pc pc}))]\n    [(image-segment space kind ident) rel]))\n\n\n(defn offset\n  \"The offset of segment `seg`'s image in kernel `k`'s code space.\"\n  [kind k seg]\n  (let [ident (if (= :stack kind) (:stack-id seg) (:register-id seg))]\n    (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/absolute-pc k [ident 0]) (register/absolute-pc k [ident 0]))\n        (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :image-not-attached {:yin.k/segment (:address seg)}))))\n\n\n(def ^:private parking-ops\n  \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow, besides a non-tail call.\"\n  #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation})\n\n\n(defn- safepoint!\n  \"Refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` unless the A instruction before `pc`\n   parks (a tail call for a tail state): a layout never invents a\n   safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  [seg pc tail?]\n  (let [t (when (and (integer? pc) (< 0 pc) (<= pc (count (:vector seg)))) (inst seg (dec pc)))\n        op (when t (nth t 0))]\n    (when-not (and t\n                   (= (body-of seg (dec pc)) (body-of seg (min pc (dec (count (:vector seg))))))\n                   (if tail?\n                     (code/tail-call? t)\n                     (or (contains? parking-ops op) (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? t))))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc pc}))))\n\n\n(defn- h->a\n  \"The A pc of H pc `hpc`; a `:push` has none and is no safepoint.\"\n  [seg hpc]\n  (or (get (:h->a seg) hpc) (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc hpc})))\n\n\n(defn- a->h\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:a->h seg) pc))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Values: positional closures and kernel continuations, both ways\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A lift context `cx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner}; a lower\n;; context `lx` adds :address, naming a canonical payload's or closure's\n;; :segment as A.\n\n(declare lift-state lower-state canonical-value positional-value)\n\n\n(defn- walk-value\n  \"`x` with its guest collections rebuilt around `f`, which converts\n   closures and continuations.\"\n  [f x]\n  (cond\n    (values/host-typed? x) (f x)\n    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk-value f k) (walk-value f v)])) x)\n    (vector? x) (mapv #(walk-value f %) x)\n    (set? x) (into #{} (map #(walk-value f %)) x)\n    (seq? x) (apply list (map #(walk-value f %) x))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-closure\n  \"§4.4: a positional closure as the canonical `{:type :closure :params\n   :entry :segment :env}`: `params` from A's `:closure` at the owner of\n   the entry body, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's frames.\"\n  [cx c]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc (:kind cx) (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:body-pc c))\n        entry (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (h->a seg rel) rel)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        _ (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-origin {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n        ps (params seg owner)]\n    (when-not (= (count ps) (:arity c))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    {:type :closure :params ps :entry entry :segment (:address seg)\n     :env (env-of seg owner (:free-env cx)\n                  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) (:frames c))\n                  (:store-of c))}))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-value*\n  [cx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner cx) (canonical-closure cx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner cx) (assoc (lift-state cx (values/payload x)) :type :reified-continuation))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn canonical-value\n  \"Kernel value `x` with every positional closure and kernel continuation\n   reconstructed canonically (cx: a lift context).\"\n  [cx x]\n  (walk-value #(canonical-value* cx %) x))\n\n\n(defn- address-of\n  [lx seg-id]\n  (let [a ((:address lx) seg-id)]\n    (when (nil? a) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment {:yin.k/hint seg-id}))\n    a))\n\n\n(defn- positional-closure\n  \"The inverse of canonical-closure: the entry's absolute pc in the\n   receiving kernel, the frames read back from `env` by the owner's chain.\"\n  [lx c]\n  (let [seg (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment c)))\n        entry (:entry c)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env c))]\n    (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)) (= (vec (:params c)) (vec (params seg owner))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    (cond-> {:type :closure :arity (count (:params c))\n             :body-pc (+ (offset (:kind lx) (:kernel lx) seg) (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (a->h seg entry) entry))\n             :frames (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg owner (:env c)))}\n      (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n\n\n(defn- positional-value*\n  [lx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner lx) (positional-closure lx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner lx) (merge {:type :reified-continuation} (lower-state lx (values/payload x))))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn positional-value\n  \"Canonical value `x` with every closure and continuation lowered into\n   the receiving kernel (lx: a lower context).\"\n  [lx x]\n  (walk-value #(positional-value* lx %) x))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lift: a kernel payload as the canonical act or tail\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private kernel-keys\n  \"The register payload keys of either kernel; every other key of an\n   entry is the engine's and travels in the pending.\"\n  #{:segment :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live :continuation :dest :resume-mode\n    :format :hash :image :images :store-of :stack})\n\n\n(defn- canonical-env\n  [cx seg pc frames store-of]\n  (env-of seg pc (:free-env cx) (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) frames) store-of))\n\n\n(defn- register-window\n  \"§4.2 step 4: `{virt(site, s) V}` over the entry's `[s V]` pairs.\"\n  [cx seg site regs]\n  (into {} (map (fn [[s x]] [(virt seg site s) (canonical-value cx x)])) regs))\n\n\n(defn- register-kframe\n  \"§4.2 step 6, one frame independently: its own image row and A, `rd`\n   from A, never the physical `:dest`.\"\n  [cx f]\n  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:return-pc f))]\n    (safepoint! seg pc false)\n    {:type :return :segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n     :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n     :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs f))\n     :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}))\n\n\n(defn- lift-register\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:pc entry))\n        tail? (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n        _ (safepoint! seg pc tail?)\n        k (mapv #(register-kframe cx %) (:continuation entry))]\n    (if tail?\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site (dec pc) :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n       :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n       :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs entry))\n       :k k\n       :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}})))\n\n\n(defn slices\n  \"§4.3's K slices of operand stack `st` under return frames whose bases\n   are `bases`, outermost first: `St[b(i-1), b(i))` per frame with\n   `b(-1) = 0`, then the current activation's `St[b(m-1), n)`.\"\n  [st bases]\n  (let [bounds (into [0] bases)]\n    (conj (mapv (fn [lo hi] (subvec st lo hi)) bounds (rest bounds))\n          (subvec st (peek bounds) (count st)))))\n\n\n(defn- slice-window\n  \"`(zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice)`; the two have equal length.\"\n  [cx seg pc slice]\n  (let [layout (stack-layout seg (dec pc))]\n    (when-not (= (count layout) (count slice))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/layout layout :yin.k/slice (count slice)}))\n    (zipmap layout (map #(canonical-value cx %) slice))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-site\n  \"`[segment site]`: the A pc of the parking instruction before absolute\n   stack resume pc `abs`.\"\n  [cx abs]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc :stack (:kernel cx) (:space cx) abs)]\n    [seg (h->a seg (dec rel))]))\n\n\n(defn- lift-stack\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [st (vec (:stack entry))\n        frames (vec (:continuation entry))\n        bases (mapv :stack-base frames)\n        _ (when-not (and (every? integer? bases) (apply <= 0 (conj bases (count st))))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-base {:yin.k/bases bases}))\n        parts (slices st bases)\n        [seg site] (stack-site cx (:pc entry))\n        tail? (code/tail-call? (inst seg site))\n        _ (safepoint! seg (inc site) tail?)\n        k (mapv (fn [f slice]\n                  (let [[fseg fsite] (stack-site cx (:return-pc f))\n                        pc (inc fsite)]\n                    (safepoint! fseg pc false)\n                    {:type :return :segment (:address fseg) :pc pc\n                     :env (canonical-env cx fseg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n                     :window (slice-window cx fseg pc slice)\n                     :rd (code/rd (inst fseg fsite))}))\n                frames parts)]\n    (if tail?\n      ;; frozen §2.4: the current frames, store-of and slice are dead\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site site :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      (let [pc (inc site)]\n        {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n         :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n         :window (slice-window cx seg pc (peek parts))\n         :k k\n         :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg site))}}))))\n\n\n(defn lift-state\n  \"A kernel's wait entry, parked record or reified payload as the\n   canonical act or tail payload (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried.\"\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [fields (apply dissoc entry kernel-keys)]\n    (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(canonical-value cx %)))\n           (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (lift-stack cx entry) (lift-register cx entry)))))\n\n\n(defn lift-context\n  \"The lift context of kernel `k` over `space`; positional closures and\n   kernel continuations are reminted under `owner`.\"\n  [k space owner]\n  (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {}) :owner owner))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lower: a canonical act or tail into a kernel\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- lower-frames\n  [lx seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg pc env)))\n\n\n(defn- payload-segment\n  [lx p]\n  (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment p))))\n\n\n(defn- lower-regs\n  \"`(sort-by first [[phys(v) V] …])` over window `w`.\"\n  [lx seg site w]\n  (vec (sort-by first (map (fn [[x v]] [(phys seg site x) (positional-value lx v)]) w))))\n\n\n(defn- register-k\n  [lx k]\n  (mapv (fn [f]\n          (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                off (offset :register (:kernel lx) seg)\n                pc (:pc f)\n                regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window f))\n                store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n            (cond-> {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :return-pc (+ off pc)\n                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                     :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n                     :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (:rd f))}\n              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n        k))\n\n\n(defn- lower-register\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :register k seg)\n        common (cond-> {:segment (:segment k) :format register/format-tag :hash (:hash k)\n                        :image (:register-id seg) :continuation (register-k lx (:k p))}\n                 (< 1 (count (:images k))) (assoc :images (:images k)))]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off (:site p)) :pc (+ off (:site p) 1)\n                             :frames (get fill :frames []) :regs [] :live [] :dest nil\n                             :resume-mode :return-result})\n        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window p))\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :pc (+ off pc)\n                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                               :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n                               :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (get-in p [:deliver :rd]))\n                               :resume-mode :write-result})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(defn- layout-slice\n  \"`(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`, every member present in W.\"\n  [lx seg pc w]\n  (mapv (fn [x]\n          (when-not (contains? w x)\n            (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :window-missing {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n          (positional-value lx (get w x)))\n        (stack-layout seg (dec pc))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-k\n  \"`[frames stack]`: each return frame's slice appended in order, its base\n   the cumulative length through its own slice (§4.3), `b(i) = b(i-1) +\n   |slice(i)|`.\"\n  [lx k]\n  (reduce (fn [[frames st] f]\n            (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                  pc (:pc f)\n                  st (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window f)))\n                  store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n              [(conj frames (cond-> {:return-pc (+ (offset :stack (:kernel lx) seg) (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                                     :stack-base (count st)}\n                              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of)))\n               st]))\n          [[] []]\n          k))\n\n\n(defn- lower-stack\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :stack k seg)\n        [frames st] (stack-k lx (:k p))\n        common {:segment (:segment k) :format stack/format-tag :hash (:hash k) :image (:stack-id seg)\n                :continuation frames}]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (:site p)) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n                             :stack st :frames (get fill :frames [])})\n        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                               :stack (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window p)))})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private canonical-keys\n  #{:segment :pc :site :env :window :k :deliver :type :id})\n\n\n(defn lower-state\n  \"Canonical act or tail payload `p` as the receiving kernel's entry\n   (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried. `fill` gives a lowered tail's\n   `:frames`/`:store-of` (default `[]`/nil), which the return transition\n   replaces unread (§5.6 item 8).\"\n  ([lx p] (lower-state lx p {}))\n  ([lx p fill]\n   (let [fields (apply dissoc p canonical-keys)]\n     (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(positional-value lx %)))\n            (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (lower-stack lx p fill) (lower-register lx p fill))))))\n\n\n(defn lower-context\n  \"The lower context into kernel `k` over `space`; `address` maps a\n   payload's or closure's `:segment` to A (default: it is A).\"\n  ([k space] (lower-context k space identity))\n  ([k space address]\n   (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {})\n          :owner (:owner k) :address address)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Tasks\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- holds-procedure?\n  \"Whether kernel value `x` is or holds, through guest collections, a\n   closure or continuation.\"\n  [x]\n  (cond\n    (or (values/closure? x) (values/continuation? x)) true\n    (values/host-typed? x) false\n    (map? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? (concat (keys x) (vals x))))\n    (coll? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? x))\n    :else false))\n\n\n(defn canonical-task\n  \"Quiescent kernel `k` presented as semantic-register machine `template`\n   (a fresh machine of the same composition) for `rows/lift-task`: code\n   by A from `space`, every wait, parked record and value reconstructed\n   through the layout; resources, stores and counters carried. A running\n   kernel is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; install children are not carried\n   by this phase's layouts; a closure or continuation in the free\n   environment has no finite canonical form (§6.3).\"\n  [k space template]\n  (when (engine/active-continuation? k)\n    (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (:pc k)}))\n  (when (seq (:installs k))\n    (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :installs {:yin.k/names (vec (keys (:installs k)))}))\n  (when-let [sym (some (fn [[sym x]] (when (holds-procedure? x) sym)) (sort-by (comp str key) (:free-env k)))]\n    (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}))\n  (let [cx (lift-context k space (:owner template))\n        conv #(canonical-value cx %)\n        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lift-state cx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n                     (:parked k))\n        value (:value k)\n        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))\n        code (into {} (map (fn [[a s]] [a (assoc {} :vector (:vector s) :address a :analysis (:analysis s))]))\n                   (:segments space))]\n    (assoc template\n           :code code\n           :code-aliases (into {} (map (fn [a] [a a])) (keys code))\n           :program nil :control nil :window {} :k nil\n           :resources (:resources k)\n           :capability-secret (:capability-secret k)\n           :free-env (:free-env k)\n           :env (or (:free-env k) {})\n           :store (conv (:store k))\n           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores k) {}))\n           :parked parked\n           :wait-set (mapv #(lift-state cx %) (:wait-set k))\n           :ready-queue (:ready-queue k)\n           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n           :id-counter (:id-counter k)\n           :installs {}\n           :halted? (:halted? k)\n           :blocked? (:blocked? k))))\n\n\n(defn lift-task\n  \"§7.1 for a de Bruijn kernel: `rows/lift-task` over its canonical\n   presentation. `template` is a fresh semantic-register machine of the\n   k","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/sto/.claude/projects/-Users-sto-workspace-datomworld-docs-6c/fc5fbdae-afe7-4b77-8da9-cb26d4729e39/tool-results/bns9eze6n.txt","persistedOutputSize":38566},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GYawGaTm8UzcmnFjL1t9KB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"subcommandResults"}}]}
