{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EyGobsmTFZYx85ETE7YUqB","type":"tool_result","content":"828\t\n829\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\n830\texpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\n831\tmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\n832\tfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\n833\tuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\n834\treason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n835\t`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n836\t\n837\t### 5.2 The matrix\n838\t\n839\t**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\n840\tunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n841\t(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\n842\tmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n843\t`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n844\t(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\n845\tpinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n846\t`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n847\t`y = :arg`). **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), one exception to the\n848\tmechanical rule (B2):** `nest`'s own lambda parameter is `x`, so under\n849\t`:depth-3` any row whose program captures the free name `x` captures the\n850\twrapper's `x` instead. Row 21 is the one such row: its `(fn [] x)` is\n851\tcreated inside the innermost `nest` application where `x = 1`, so the\n852\treference run itself answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]`, not `[3 [2 [1 99]]]`, and\n853\tthe harness pins `1` for that one case (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc`,\n854\tthe `(and (= 21 n) (= :depth-3 label))` branch). The bare, `:operand`\n855\tand `:captured` results of row 21 keep `99`. The wrapper rule is\n856\totherwise unchanged; the exception is stated rather than the wrapper\n857\trenamed, because the old harness's segments are shared and the lowered\n858\trun matches the reference either way (law 1 holds on the row). **Frames\n859\tthe wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\n860\ttrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\n861\tdefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\n862\tis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\n863\tnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\n864\toutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\n865\tthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n866\t(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n867\t`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\n868\tadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n869\t`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n870\t`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\n871\tthe wrappers.\n872\t\n873\tPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n874\t`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\n875\tof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n876\t`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n877\t(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\n878\treference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\n879\ton all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\n880\tjudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\n881\tlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n882\t\n883\t| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n884\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n885\t| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n886\t| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n887\t| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n888\t| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n889\t| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n890\t| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n891\t| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n892\t| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n893\t| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n894\t| 10 | install, blocked child (**amended after 6b**) | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, **phase `:parked`** | `'host.mod` | yes |\n895\t| 11 | install, `:running` wire phase (**amended after 6b, synthetic phase**) | row 10's machine with the install entry's phase set to `:running` **by hand** before the lift | as 10 | act, `:install`, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | the program is row 10's; the phase is not engine-reachable at a safepoint (see below) |\n896\t| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n897\t| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n898\t| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n899\t| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n900\t| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n901\t| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | yes; **amended after 6b:** the receiver composition is given the source's registry entry for `host.mod`, a composition prerequisite (§2.5): linked registry entries do not travel |\n902\t| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes; **amended after 6b:** the fixture's module store is hand-built and keyed by the **symbol** `host.mod`, as `frame_test` does, while the kernel keys installed stores by module address; row 17 exercises a real installation |\n903\t| 19 | halt | `(lit 42)` | — | result | `42` | yes |\n904\t| 20 | **tail-effect halt** | run **outside the wrappers**, in two batches on one machine: a setup batch `(then (def! 's (stream/make 4)) (def! 'c (cursor-of s)))` run to halt (store writes persist across batches, as `lift_support.cljc:320-322` relies on), then the batch `(stream/next! c)` **marked `:tail? true` at the root**, `c` resolving through the store; no application precedes the call, so `K` is empty (any `let1` or wrapper would push a frame, §5.2 above) | append `\"B\"` | tail, `:next` | halts with `\"B\"` (4b: a tail effect with no frame to pop halts, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:205-211` stage 2; stack: the seam's empty-`K` case, §4.3), env free of `store-of` | yes |\n905\t| 21 | **free-env shadowing** (the review's row) | initial free env `{x 99}`; `(let1 f (fn [] x) ((fn [x] (f (next-of c))) 1))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `E` at the read binds `x` to 1; root `free-env` carries `x 99` | the lowered `f` returns `99` (not nil, not 1) on every lane; **the one row the earlier design would have failed** | yes |\n906\t| 22 | nil-valued live register | `(let1 pair (fn [p q] (cat (cat [] p) q)) (pair nil (next-of c)))`: the `nil` operand is evaluated **before** the read and is live across it (`(cat nil (next-of c))` would give `(conj nil \"B\")`, the list `(\"B\")`, so a vector-building `pair` is used; there is no `vector` primitive, `vm.cljc:380-410`, and `pair` builds through the segment's `cat`) | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `W` has a present key with `[:lit nil]` | `[nil \"B\"]` | yes |\n907\t| 23 | tail FFI bookkeeping | a `tail` parked record under a call id `P ∈ :ffi-call-ids` | — | parked, context `:parked`, bookkeeping clause of P4 | validator accepts; the **same valid tail under a key not in `:ffi-call-ids`** is `:parked-site` (P4); moving `site` to a non-tail call is `:site` (T1, which runs first) | **synthetic**: no primitive returns an FFI effect through a tail `:call` today (evaluator §1.6) |\n908\t| 24 | `act` FFI bookkeeping | 3's parked bookkeeping record under its call id | — | parked, P4 bookkeeping | validator accepts | 4b stage 2 |\n909\t\n910\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), rows 10-12 and the install contract\n911\t(B1).** The earlier text promised a child \"`:blocked`, phase `:running`\"\n912\t(row 10) and a child \"executing `:park`, kind `:parked`\" (row 11).\n913\tNeither is an engine state at a quiescent safepoint. `advance-install`\n914\t(`engine.cljc:1421-1454`) runs the child inside the sweep and then\n915\trecords it: a **blocked** child is retained at phase **`:parked`**\n916\t(`:1444-1446`); a **halted** child goes straight to `link-install` in the\n917\tsame sweep (`:1447-1452`), so it is never retained; and a child that\n918\texecutes `:park` is `:halted?` with a parked value, so it too goes to\n919\t`link-install`, which completes the install if the exports are already\n920\tdefined and otherwise refuses it `:export-missing`. Phase `:running` is\n921\tassigned only in the `:else` arm, within one sweep, and is overwritten\n922\tbefore any safepoint. So: row 10 is the engine's real state (child\n923\t`:blocked`, phase `:parked`); row 11 exercises the `:running` **wire\n924\tphase**, which the codec accepts (`install-node` admits `#{:running\n925\t:parked}` on lift and `check-install!` on lower) and the harness\n926\tconstructs by hand on row 10's machine (`installing % false :running`,\n927\t`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:342`); row 12 is row 10's child under tail\n928\tdelivery. No row promises an executable kind-`:parked` install child on\n929\tthe current engine (owner question §11 Q1). **The install validation\n930\tthe lower enforces** (`check-install!`, before any attachment, §7.2):\n931\tthe phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\n932\tretained response is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` map whose\n933\t`:yin.module/name` equals the install's module name and whose\n934\t`:yin.module/exports` is a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map\n935\twhose `:value` the receiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`);\n936\tand the carried child task's kind is **`:blocked`** (`:install-child`),\n937\tbecause a halted or parked child would have been linked in the sweep it\n938\thalted in. On lift, `install-node` refuses a phase outside the set as\n939\t`:yin.k/non-portable :incomplete-install` and a response CBOR cannot\n940\tencode as `:yin.k/non-portable :install-response`.\n941\t\n942\tRefusal rows: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`,\n943\t`:591-603`); `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one\n944\tinstruction past a `:const`, asked to lift); `:yin.k/non-portable\n945\t:reason-mismatch` (the `:observe` hold, `:675-692`); and the validator's\n946\t**membership tampers**, each a row-set edit of a valid body before\n947\tlower: a window with an extra live key → `:window-extra`; a live key\n948\tremoved → `:window-missing`; `rd` added → `:window-self`; `rd` replaced\n949\tby another register → `:destination`; a return frame's `pc` moved after\n950\ta tail call → `:return-site`; a reason the site does not admit →\n951\t`:reason-mismatch`; a `:tail` row given a fourth slot → **`:row-shape`\n952\tat the codec's structure step (2), not `:frame-shape`** (amended after\n953\t6b, B3: the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots, so the wire never\n954\treaches rule S1 with a fourth slot; S1 still governs an abstract frame\n955\thanded to `frame/defect` directly; and because `emit`/`emit-numbered`\n956\tsilently drop extra slots from an abstract node, mapping over the\n957\tshorter descriptor list, this tamper must be a **raw row edit with a\n958\trehash**, never an abstract-task edit);\n959\tthe `[:lit nil]` of row 22 dropped → `:window-missing`; a `:reified`\n960\tact whose `pc−1` is not a capture → `:captured-site`; a parked act whose\n961\t`pc−1` is not `:park` → `:parked-site`; for E2, with row 21's initial\n962\tfree environment extended to `{x 99 y 7}`, the **unshadowed** free\n963\tbinding `y` in the act's `E` changed to `8` → `:free-env-mismatch`\n964\t(changing the lexical `x`, which is `1` at the read, trips nothing: E2\n965\tcompares off the lexical names), and the lexical `x` **removed** from\n966\t`E` → `:free-env-mismatch` (presence clause (a)); the root `free-env`\n967\trow given a reserved key → `:reserved-name` (X1). **A changed live value**\n968\t(e.g. `\"A\"` → `\"Z\"` in row 1's window) is not a membership defect and is\n969\tnot expected to be refused: it is tested by **execution parity** (the\n970\tlowered run's result differs from the reference), as the review ruled.\n971\t\n972\t### 5.3 Lanes\n973\t\n974\tThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\n975\tengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\n976\tand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\n977\tthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\n978\tthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n979\t9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n980\t\n981\t### 5.4 Laws\n982\t\n983\t1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n984\t   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n985\t   `:368-401`).\n986\t2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n987\t   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n988\t   §5.5.\n989\t3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n990\t   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n991\t4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n992\t   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n993\t   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n994\t5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n995\t   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n996\t   reference result.\n997\t6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n998\t   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n999\t   verdict is lane-independent.\n1000\t\n1001\t### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n1002\t\n1003\t**PROPOSED** (reviewer: `stream-blind` rewrites decoded maps and does\n1004\tnot recompute Merkle ids, so it cannot establish root-id equality). The\n1005\tharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\n1006\ttheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n1007\t`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\n1008\tlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\n1009\tlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\n1010\tpair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\n1011\ttransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\n1012\t*host* traversal order** (the canonical walk of draft §6 is a function\n1013\tof the state and is what the procedure reads; the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n1014\t\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\n1015\ttakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\n1016\tthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n1017\t`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n1018\t:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\n1019\tabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\n1020\tand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\n1021\tdoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\n1022\tcursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\n1023\tand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n1024\t**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\n1025\tother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's body\n1026\tlabel (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as\n1027\tamended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\n1028\tlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\n1029\tidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\n1030\ttheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\n1031\tthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\n1032\tenumeration order, so\n1033\ta permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\n1034\tthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n1035\t\n1036\t### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n1037\t\n1038\t1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n1039\t2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n1040\t   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n1041\t3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n1042\t   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n1043\t   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n1044\t   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n1045\t   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n1046\t   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n1047\t   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n1048\t4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n1049\t   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n1050\t5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n1051\t   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n1052\t6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n1053\t   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n1054\t   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n1055\t   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n1056\t7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n1057\t   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n1058\t   `:duplicate-param`.\n1059\t8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n1060\t   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\n1061\t   values gives equal observables on rows 6, 9, 12, 20.\n1062\t9. Slices: for every non-tail safepoint with `m` return frames, the\n1063\t   `m+1` slices of §4.3 partition the stack kernel's operand stack\n1064\t   exactly, and each has the length of its `stack-layout`.\n1065\t\n1066\t---\n1067\t\n1068\t## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n1069\t\n1070\t### 6.1 Code\n1071\t\n1072\t**DECIDED** (rulings 1, 3, 4; phase-5 §7.6, §9.2; v2 amendment §4's\n1073\t2026-10-10 note). The root's `code` slot is plain `{A canonical-vector}`\n1074\tof `\"v4\"` vectors, verified to hash to their key and to pass\n1075\t`code/well-formed?`. **No native image travels**: a stack or register\n1076\treceiver derives `H(A)`/`R(A)` with `derive/stack-image`/`register-image`.\n1077\t`requires` carries UCF §7.6.1's map **plus** `:yin.k/trees {A #{tree-address …}}`,\n1078\tone entry per address in `:yin.k/segments`; the **complete association\n1079\trule** is phase-5's: an absent entry **or an empty set** is incomplete\n1080\t(`:yin.k/missing :yin.k/trees`, discovery `:incomplete`), and a claimed\n1081\taddress alone does not establish retrievability (a known address whose\n1082\tread fails now is the medium's `:absent`, not a missing association).\n1083\t\n1084\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), where the association comes from.**\n1085\tThe kernel holds no source index, so the tree association is a **lift\n1086\tinput**: `(rows/lift-task m serve! {:source-index {A #{tree-address …}}})`\n1087\tfrom the composition (the harness records `A → segment-key [:yin/tree\n1088\tast]` whenever it projects a vector). After the walk has fixed the\n1089\tcarried segments, the lift refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` (`:yin.k/discovery\n1090\t:incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/trees #{A …}}`) for any carried\n1091\tsegment whose entry is absent or empty; the lower repeats the same check\n1092\tover `requires`' `:yin.k/trees` against `code`'s keys\n1093\t(`check-dependencies!`). No source-index slot is persisted in the\n1094\tcarrier. *Why:* engineer deviation 1, accepted by codex: the index\n1095\tbelongs to the composition, not the machine.\n1096\t\n1097\t### 6.2 Rows, not datoms\n1098\t\n1099\t**DECIDED** (ruling 4). No vector is projected to datoms; the direct\n1100\tload path is the only path (§10.4).\n1101\t\n1102\t### 6.3 The free environment: carrier, source, ownership\n1103\t\n1104\t**DECIDED** by the review's finding and §4.4. The task's initial free\n1105\tenvironment travels **once, explicitly**, as the root's `free-env`\n1106\tslot, a `:bindings` row (`[S V …]` sorted). Every `E` in the task is a\n1107\tcomplete map and is checked by rules X1 and E2. On lower, the\n1108\tsemantic-register kernel receives it as the machine's base `:env`\n1109\t(`create-vm`'s `:env` option, `semantic_register.cljc:261`); a de Bruijn\n1110\tkernel receives it as its `:free-env` (`stack.cljc:418`) and\n1111\treconstructs positional frames from each `E` by the chain (§4.4).\n1112\t\n1113\t**Where the lift gets it (PROPOSED, the gap the review named).** It is\n1114\t**never recovered from a current `E`** (§4.4). Its source is, per\n1115\tkernel:\n1116\t\n1117\t- **de Bruijn kernels**: the kernel's own `:free-env` field, \"fixed for\n1118\t  this instance\" (`stack.cljc:82`; `register.cljc:50`), read directly.\n1119\t- **the semantic-register kernel**: the evaluator keeps only the\n1120\t  mutable-current `:env` (`RegisterVM` field `env`, \"persistent lexical\n1121\t  scope map\", `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:23`; the old semantic\n1122\t  VM likewise captures the initial env only transiently in `eval`,\n1123\t  `semantic.cljc:874-876`), so no field holds the initial map. The\n1124\t  **composition owns it**: it is the value the composition passed as\n1125\t  `create-vm`'s `:env` (`vm/check-bindings!` of it, `:261`). The lift's\n1126\t  input contract therefore takes it explicitly,\n1127\t  `(rows/lift-task vm serve! {:free-env E₀})`, and refuses\n1128\t  `:yin.k/non-portable :missing-free-env` when the caller supplies none;\n1129\t  the holder and REPL paths that call the lift pass the environment they\n1130\t  constructed the task with. **Coordinated retention seam, recommended**:\n1131\t  4b (stage 3, its track's file) adds an immutable `free-env` field set\n1132\t  once in `create-vm` from the checked `:env` and never written by any\n1133\t  transition, with `gc-roots` listing it; once it lands the lift reads\n1134\t  it when `:free-env` is not supplied and the explicit argument stays as\n1135\t  the override for compositions that constructed the task elsewhere.\n1136\t  Phase 6 does **not** edit `semantic_register.cljc`; 6b depends on the\n1137\t  explicit argument and names the seam as a follow-up (§8.2).\n1138\t\n1139\t  **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the seam has landed and the lift reads\n1140\t  it.** The 4b track added the immutable `free-env` field\n1141\t  (`semantic_register.cljc:24`, set once in `create-vm` `:394`, a gc root\n1142\t  `:489`). `lift-task*` takes `(:free-env opts)` when supplied, else the\n1143\t  kernel's `(:free-env m)`, and refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n1144\t  :missing-free-env` only when neither exists. The explicit value\n1145\t  **overrides** the field without comparing against it; an override the\n1146\t  carried `E`s disagree with is caught by E2 when the lift validates its\n1147\t  own frames (`:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state`, e.g. `{x 5}`\n1148\t  against an `E` holding `x 99` off the lexical names). Install children\n1149\t  are lifted with the `:free-env` option removed, so a child always reads\n1150\t  its own kernel field.\n1151\t\n1152\tMerkle sharing means every `E` whose bindings equal another's shares one\n1153\t`:bindings` row; the root slot is one more reference to a row that\n1154\tusually already exists.\n1155\t\n1156\t---\n1157\t\n1158\t## 7. Lift and lower\n1159\t\n1160\t### 7.1 Lift\n1161\t\n1162\t**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): refuse unless quiescent; refuse the\n1163\tholds (`unliftable-hold`, `handoff.cljc:588`) as `:yin.k/non-portable\n1164\t:reason-mismatch`; classify kind; run the **dependency closure over the\n1165\t`\"v4\"` vectors**, which needs the phase-5 scanners\n1166\t(`scan/requirements`, `scan/free-names`, `scan/free-occurrences` over the\n1167\t§2.3 table: the current `completion/code-facts` reads stack-table\n1168\tpositions and cannot be used), and refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied` unless\n1169\t`:complete`, `:yin.k/trees` included; number cursor cells (draft §6); walk\n1170\twaits, parked records, the store slice, module stores, the free\n1171\tenvironment (from the kernel's `:free-env` field or the lift's explicit\n1172\t`:free-env` argument, §6.3, never from a current `E`), the result,\n1173\tinstall children; run `frame/defect` over every\n1174\tframe; emit rows and the census. A de Bruijn source walks the frames\n1175\t`layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail` reconstruct.\n1176\t\n1177\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the order as `lift-task*` runs it (B4),\n1178\tand the discharge rule.** The paragraph above put the dependency closure\n1179\tbefore the numbering and the walk, but the carried segments, contexts\n1180\tand module stores are known only once the walk has found them, so the\n1181\tclosure cannot run first. The merged order is:\n1182\t\n1183\t1. **Quiescence and safepoint.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a\n1184\t   non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds (`unliftable-hold`: `:observe`,\n1185\t   `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`,\n1186\t   `:link-cursor-not-installed`) as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`;\n1187\t   refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set.\n1188\t2. **Kind.** `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked\n1189\t   table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted`\n1190\t   with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`.\n1191\t3. **Free environment** (§6.3): the option, else the kernel field, else\n1192\t   `:missing-free-env`.\n1193\t4. **The walk.** Waits (state and pending), the parked table, the whole\n1194\t   store, the result (halted only), install children (each a recursive\n1195\t   `lift-task*` in the child role with its own accumulator), then the\n1196\t   module stores by reachability. Every value is classified `[:lit x]`,\n1197\t   `[:row n]` or `[:cursor-cell source]`; references are authenticated\n1198\t   against the kernel's resource table before encoding\n1199\t   (`:forged-resource-reference`), a foreign-owned value is\n1200\t   `:foreign-value`, a heap cell `:cell`, a host object `:host-object`.\n1201\t   The walk records every **segment** reached and every **context**: a\n1202\t   closure's `(A, entry, E)`, an `act`'s `(A, pc, E)`, a return frame's\n1203\t   `(A, pc, E)`. A `tail` state contributes its `K` frames' contexts and\n1204\t   none for its own dead activation.\n1205\t5. **Code.** `{A vector}` for the segments reached, from the kernel's\n1206\t   code table through its aliases; an unaddressed segment is\n1207\t   `:yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment`, an address the kernel\n1208\t   cannot produce a vector for is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n1209\t6. **Dependency closure** (`discharge!`), **per context, over live\n1210\t   code.** For each recorded context `(A, at, E)`, the live pcs are\n1211\t   `(live-pcs v at)`: the closure of `at` under `analysis/successors`\n1212\t   plus, for every reachable `:closure` instruction, that closure's body\n1213\t   from its entry (reachable code can still construct it). Every free\n1214\t   occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) at a live pc must be **answered\n1215\t   in that context**, in `resolve-var`'s order: a reserved name; the\n1216\t   context's own `E`; the **one** store `engine/active-store` selects for\n1217\t   that `E` (the root store when `E` has no `store-of`, else the module\n1218\t   store it names, both of which travel); a profiled primitive (a\n1219\t   `:host`-class profile refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n1220\t   :host-state-primitive` at that point, so a host primitive shadowed by\n1221\t   `E` or the store lifts); an installing module's export (`m/x` built\n1222\t   with `(symbol (name m) (name x))`); a module the registry resolves for\n1223\t   a namespaced name. Any unanswered name is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n1224\t   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:obligations #{…}}`.\n1225\t   *Why per context:* codex F3, the union of all module-store keys\n1226\t   answered names the running closure could not resolve. *Why live\n1227\t   code:* codex F6, a numeric or whole-body scan refused valid\n1228\t   continuations whose unreachable branches named a missing symbol.\n1229\t   The parser admits no backward jump (§3.1 of the frozen design), so no\n1230\t   carried vector exercises the backward edge; `live-pcs` is public and\n1231\t   is tested directly on a hand-built vector with a `:jump` to an earlier\n1232\t   pc. Discharge runs at lift only; the lower does not re-discharge free\n1233\t   names against the receiver (§7.2).\n1234\t7. **Trees** (§6.1): every carried segment has a non-empty entry in the\n1235\t   `:source-index` option, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees`.\n1236\t8. **The task.** `requires` is `{:yin.k/cursor-profiles … :yin.k/segments\n1237\t   … :yin.k/trees …}`; the root is assembled with `custody nil` (§2.5).\n1238\t9. **Frames.** `validate-frames` over the assembled abstract task (X1,\n1239\t   every wait, parked state and reified value, children recursively); a\n1240\t   defect is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` carrying it. The\n1241\t   lift does not decode its own output a second time.\n1242\t10. **Emit** (`emit`): canonical cursor-cell numbering and rows.\n1243\t\n1244\t**Refusals are thrown.** Every refusal of lift and lower is an `ex-info`\n1245\twhose ex-data carries `:yin.k/status`, a `:yin.k/kind` where one is\n1246\tnamed, and the defect's data, the same convention as `decode` and `emit`;\n1247\tnothing returns a `{:status …}` map (engineer deviation 2, accepted).\n1248\t\n1249\t### 7.2 Lower\n1250\t\n1251\t**DECIDED** (draft §8 in order; §3.3): gate, structure, graph, hashes,\n1252\tcanonical form, canonical numbering, **frames**, pending and operations,\n1253\tdependency closure (`requires`/`code` agree with the census;\n1254\t`:yin.k/trees` complete per §6.1), custody, composition, restore. Restore\n1255\tbuilds the receiving kernel's state: semantic-register: `code` through\n1256\t`load-vector`/`attach-image` under `code/contract`, the free environment\n1257\tas base `:env`, each `:wait` to an engine entry (payload plus the\n1258\tpending's engine fields, `lower-frame!`'s shapes, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`,\n1259\t`:deliver` carried), parked records into `:parked`, the result into\n1260\t`:value`; de Bruijn: derive and attach the image for each A, set\n1261\t`:free-env`, and `layout/lower-act`/`lower-tail` each frame (the stack\n1262\tlane's `tail` needs 6c-pre). Restoration delivers nothing, runs nothing,\n1263\tmints nothing.\n1264\t\n1265\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the lower's staged checks as\n1266\t`lower-task` runs them, and its refusal kinds.** Steps 1-7 are `decode`\n1267\t(gate, structure, graph, hashes, canonical form, numbering, frames,\n1268\t§3.3). Steps 8-10 then run as four stages, **each over the whole task\n1269\ttree (root and every install child, pre-order) before the next**, and\n1270\t**every one of them before the first `attach!`**; a defect in a child's\n1271\tlater stage therefore cannot be masked by the root's earlier one, and\n1272\tzero streams are attached on any refusal (codex F1, F2, F4, F7; mutants\n1273\tthat attach early are killed by the attach-count law):\n1274\t\n1275\t| Stage | Function | Checks, in order | Refusal |\n1276\t|---|---|---|---|\n1277\t| stores | `check-stores!` | no key of the root store or of any module store is a reserved name (`vm/reserved-name?`), refused here rather than by restore's `store-put` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name` (`:yin.k/role :store-key`) |\n1278\t| pendings and install records | `check-pendings!` | the task's role slot equals its position (`:root`/`:child`); every `:install` pending names a carried install row; kind consistency (`:halted` has no waits, `:blocked` has waits, `:parked` names a key of its parked table); then every install record by `check-install!` (§5.2: phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}`, the retained response and image contract, the child `:blocked`) | `:role`, `:incomplete-install`, `:kind`, `:parked-id`, `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`, all `:yin.k/undecodable` |\n1279\t| dependencies | `check-dependencies!` | `requires` is a map whose `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code` (`:requires`); every segment has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry; every `store-of` of every `:closure` and `:env` row of **this task** (children excluded, each checked in its own stage pass) names one of this task's `:module-store` rows (§2.6), so a parent's or sibling's store of the same key cannot answer (codex F7, F8); every vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`attaches?`: `module/attach-module` into a throwaway copy, the result discarded) (`:segment`); every `:closure` row names a `:closure` tuple of its vector at its entry (`:closure-origin`) with the same binders (`:closure-binders`); every `:prim` names a primitive the receiver holds | `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees` or **`:yin.k/stores`** (`:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`), `:yin.k/undecodable :segment`, `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/name p}` |\n1280\t| custody | `check-custody!` | the root's custody slot is nil (phase 6 carries fork only) | `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` |\n1281\t\n1282\tThen **attachment** (step 11, `attach-streams!`): one `attach!` per\n1283\tdistinct stream identity of the whole tree, `:yin.k/unsatisfied\n1284\t{:dao.stream/identity …}` when the composition declines. Then **restore**\n1285\t(step 12, `restore-task`): the carried vectors attached by A into a\n1286\treceiver emptied of code, so frame and closure addresses map through the\n1287\treceiver's own aliases and no source-local segment number travels; fresh\n1288\tresource keys per stream identity and per cursor cell; values re-sealed\n1289\tunder the receiver's owner; waits in `lower-frame!`'s entry shapes, a\n1290\t`tail` with `{:deliver :return}` and no window, env or pc; parked records\n1291\twith a cycle guard; the store through `engine/store-put`; module stores;\n1292\tinstall children spawned by the engine's spawn discipline and then\n1293\trestored recursively (the spawn's initial control is replaced, no\n1294\t`vm/run`); the free environment as both `:env` and `:free-env`; the id\n1295\tcounter `max(local, carried)`. Restore meets no carrier defect, by\n1296\tconstruction of the stages above. The lower does **not** re-discharge\n1297\tfree names against the receiver: a name a closure resolves through the\n1298\tregistry is the composition prerequisite of §2.5, and the receiver's\n1299\tregistry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n1300\t:kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n1301\t:install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\n1302\tcensus are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\n1303\tnamed none of them (deviation 9, accepted with the requirement that every\n1304\tdefect be a named transport outcome before attachment).\n1305\t\n1306\t---\n1307\t\n1308\t## 8. Slices, gates, entry conditions\n1309\t\n1310\tVerification follows `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: G0 per iteration on\n1311\tthe JVM, G1 `bb test:sub yin.vm` on three hosts, G2 `bb test:changed`,\n1312\tG3 on master.\n1313\t\n1314\t### 8.1 Slice 6a: the frame validator and the row codec (independent)\n1315\t\n1316\t**Files to create.**\n1317\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc` (§3): `defect`, public.\n1318\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (§2): the row table as data,\n1319\t  `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`, row ids (Jing default), the census and\n1320\t  numbering per the draft §6, `canonical-under` (§5.5, test-only but\n1321\t  placed here so three test namespaces share it). **Not** `lift-task`/\n1322\t  `lower-task`: full task transport is 6b.\n1323\t- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame_test.cljc`,\n1324\t  `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`.\n1325\t\n1326\t**Depends on:** phase 3, 4a (landed), 4b stage 1 (branch) for live\n1327\tframes to validate. Touches nothing of 4b or 5; needs no scanner.\n1328\t\n1329\t**Acceptance (6a).**\n1330\t\n1331\t1. `frame-test/contract-is-pure`: `frame/defect` takes `[frame code\n1332\t   context task]`, `task` being plain data; the namespace's `ns` form\n1333\t   requires no kernel and no `yin.vm.engine` (a string check over the\n1334\t   source on the JVM lane, `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`).\n1335\t2. `frame-test/every-reference-frame-validates`: for every 4b-stage-1\n1336\t   row of §5.2 (1, 2, 5, 6, 13-16, 18, 19, 21, 22) over the four segments,\n1337\t   plus row 20 as one separate, unwrapped run (its empty `K` needs no wrapper\n1338\t   frames; §8.2 6b makes the same exception), every\n1339\t   `act`, `tail`, return frame, parked record and reified value the\n1340\t   register evaluator mints passes `defect` in its context with the\n1341\t   task context decoded from the machine's own tables.\n1342\t3. `frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper`: for each rule S1-S4, C1-C3,\n1343\t   A1-A6, T1, K1-K3, E1-E2, X1, P1-P4 one tampered frame built from a\n1344\t   valid one, refused with that rule's kind and a path naming the slot\n1345\t   (**twenty-four rules**: 4+3+6+1+3+2+1+4, the twenty-three of the\n1346\t   round-1 text plus X1; **twenty-five rows**, since C2 contributes two,\n1347\t   a float `pc` for `:pc-kind` and an out-of-range one for `:pc-bounds`;\n1348\t   X1's row goes through `frame/task-defect`); plus\n1349\t   `first-defect-is-structural`: a frame with a malformed `K` **and** a\n1350\t   bad window reports `:k-shape`.\n1351\t4. `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member`: row 22's frame\n1352\t   validates with the nil key present; the same frame with the key\n1353\t   removed is `:window-missing`.\n1354\t5. `frame-test/worked-example-saved`: on `:worked-example`, a frame at pc\n1355\t   4 with window `{1 <f>}` and `rd 2` validates; `{1 <f> 2 x}` is\n1356\t   `:window-self`; `{}` is `:window-missing`; `{1 <f> 3 x}` is\n1357\t   `:window-extra` (`saved(4, 2) = {1}`, `analysis_test.cljc:131-139`).\n1358\t6. `frame-test/free-env-mismatch`: row 21's frame (initial free\n1359\t   environment `{x 99 y 7}`) with `task :free-env {x 99 y 7}` validates;\n1360\t   with the free environment `{x 99 y 8}` or `{x 99}` it is\n1361\t   `:free-env-mismatch` (`y` is unshadowed); with the lexical `x` removed\n1362\t   from `E` it is `:free-env-mismatch` (presence); changing the lexical\n1363\t   `x` in `E` from `1` to `98` **does not** trip E2 (it is off the\n1364\t   comparison), and the test asserts that too; a `free-env` row with the\n1365\t   key `yin/def` is `:reserved-name` (X1).\n1366\t7. `rows-test/bijection`: `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame` and the converse\n1367\t   are identities on every frame of item 2, nil-valued keys included.\n1368\t8. `rows-test/row-ids-are-jing-default`: every content row's id equals\n1369\t   `(jing/segment-key body)` and names Jing's default algorithm; no\n1370\t   `sha256` appears in any id.\n1371\t9. `rows-test/window-sorted-and-unique`: an out-of-order or duplicated\n1372\t   `:window` row is `:yin.k/undecodable` at canonical form (step 5).\n1373\t10. `rows-test/canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`: §5.5's\n1374\t    transformation gives equal root ids for two emissions of one abstract\n1375\t    task under permuted cursor-cell numbering and distinct stream identities,\n1376\t    with the fixture's correspondence map; **and the unordered-container\n1377\t    permutation**: a store value that is a set of two stream references\n1378\t    and a map keyed by two cursor references, emitted twice with the two\n1379\t    streams' identities swapped (and the correspondence map swapped\n1380\t    accordingly), gives equal root ids; a marker absent from the map is\n1381\t    a test failure.\n1382\t11. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log\n1383\t    shows `Testing yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test` and\n1384\t    `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n1385\t12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n1386\t    the maps of the numbering note's appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x\n1387\t    :y`, the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`,\n1388\t    the witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and\n1389\t    the two frame fixtures.\n1390\t13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n1391\t    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…`; the path\n1392\t    pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned `d84480d4…`; each equal\n1393\t    after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n1394\t14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n1395\t    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n1396\t    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n1397\t    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n1398\t15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n1399\t    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n1400\t    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…`; the witness numbers `B cc-1,\n1401\t    A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…`, and decodes; the balanced-alias witness\n1402\t    `#{[X X Y Y] [Z W Z W]}` gives one root for both enumerations, pinned\n1403\t    `5376f133…`, and the key without the local index would not.\n1404\t16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n1405\t    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n1406\t    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored, the two-2-cycle and\n1407\t    the 3-cycle-beside-fan states refuse at `emit` with\n1408\t    `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of\n1409\t    the numbering note's appendix A, the same verdict and path after\n1410\t    renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of each built with `emit-numbered`\n1411\t    decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the\n1412\t    path prefixed by the root, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a\n1413\t    parent whose install `'a` holds the 2-cycle refuses at `emit` with\n1414\t    path `[12 a 15]` and its `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the\n1415\t    `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n1416\t17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n1417\t    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n1418\t    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n1419\t    `55ff168e…` removed.\n1420\t18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (at least 2000\n1421\t    random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with install children)\n1422\t    in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its\n1423\t    root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and\n1424\t    census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict and path;\n1425\t    refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is reported.\n1426\t19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n1427\t    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n1428\t20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n1429\t    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n1430\t    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n1431\t    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n1432\t    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged against the\n1433\t    rewritten decoder; `numbering-precedes-frame-defect` is extended: the\n1434\t    2-cycle with the same reserved-name binding, carried by\n1435\t    `emit-numbered`, decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n1436\t    :unresolved-cursor-cells`, not `:reserved-name`.\n1437\t21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n1438\t    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n1439\t    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n1440\t    `[:depth]`, and so do one cell inside 3000 nested vectors under\n1441\t    `{:depth 3}` and under the default budget, and a literal nested 3000\n1442\t    deep beside a cell (no host stack overflow on any of the three hosts;\n1443\t    200 nested vectors number); a set holding a 400000-scalar literal\n1444\t    refuses `[:work]` under `{:work 2000}` with the work counter at 2001\n1445\t    and under `{:work 900000}` with at most 1000 estimator steps; a state\n1446\t    whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor numbers under the\n1447\t    default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at `emit` and, for a\n1448\t    valid carrier, at `decode`.\n1449\t22. Budgets: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default\n1450\t    `4194304`.\n1451\t\n1452\t### 8.2 Slice 6b: full task transport and the harness on the reference lane\n1453\t\n1454\t**Files to change.** `rows.cljc`: `lift-task`, `lower-task` (§7), the\n1455\tpending rows' lift and lower, install children. **Files to create.**\n1456\t`test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (§5, the\n1457\t`:semantic-register` lane). The old harness file is not edited.\n1458\t\n1459\t**Depends on:** the explicit `:free-env` lift argument supplied by the\n1460\tharness (§6.3; the 4b retention seam is a named follow-up for the 4b\n1461\ttrack, not a 6b prerequisite; **amended after 6b:** the seam landed\n1462\tbefore 6b and the lift reads the field by default); the composition's\n1463\t`:source-index` (§6.1); 4b stage 2 (FFI retained→sent restoration, link\n1464\trequest/response/install preservation, install children, the\n1465\tevaluator's `IModuleKernel` linking methods: `link-format`,\n1466\t`spawn-module`, `lift-closure`, `lower-closure`); **phase 5c** (the\n1467\t`scan` namespace, for the dependency closure over `\"v4\"` vectors, §7.1).\n1468\tTouches nothing of 4b or 5.\n1469\t\n1470\t**Acceptance (6b).**\n1471\t\n1472\t1. `every-row-every-segment-matches-the-reference`: rows 1-19, 21, 22\n1473\t   and 24 of §5.2 × four segments, plus row 20 once (outside the\n1474\t   wrappers, §5.2), on the reference lane; laws 1-3 and 6; counts\n1475\t   asserted (22 wrapped rows × 4 segments + 1 = 89 runs).\n1476\t2. `tail-states-carry-no-window`: rows 6, 9, 12, 20 lift to `:tail`\n1477\t   rows of three slots and lower to through-`K` delivery.\n1478\t3. `install-rows-carry-the-child`: rows 10-12; the child's kind and\n1479\t   phase as stated **in the amended rows (10: `:blocked`/`:parked`; 11:\n1480\t   `:blocked`/`:running` set by hand; 12: row 10's child, tail\n1481\t   delivery)**; no initialization on lower (the restored child has nil\n1482\t   `:control`, is still blocked on its one `:next`, and its code is\n1483\t   attached by A).\n1484\t4. `captures`: rows 14-16 round-trip; row 15's invocation after lower\n1485\t   delivers once into the captured `rd` and yields `:done`.\n1486\t5. `free-env-shadowing`: row 21 yields `99` after lower.\n1487\t6. `synthetic-tail-bookkeeping`: row 23's hand-built parked `tail`\n1488\t   under a call id in `:ffi-call-ids` passes `defect` with context\n1489\t   `:parked`; the **same valid record under a key absent from\n1490\t   `:ffi-call-ids`** is `:parked-site` (P4); the record with `site` moved\n1491\t   to a non-tail call is `:site` (T1 runs before P4).\n1492\t7. `refusals`: every refusal row of §5.2 refuses with the named status\n1493\t   and kind before any attachment (**amended after 6b:** the `:tail`\n1494\t   fourth slot is `:row-shape`, B3; the attach count is asserted to be\n1495\t   zero on every refusal and equal to the number of distinct stream\n1496\t   identities on every success, the law that kills the early-attach and\n1497\t   duplicate-attach mutants); `first-defect-order`: a body with a\n1498\t   noncanonical cursor-cell numbering **and** a tampered window reports\n1499\t   `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n1500\t8. `a-response-cursor-shared-by-two-waiters-crosses-as-one-cursor-cell` and\n1501\t   `a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`, ported.\n1502\t9. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts.\n1503\t\n1504\t### 8.3 Slice 6c: the layouts, the stack seam, the cross-engine lanes\n1505\t\n1506\t**6c-pre, the stack tail-completion seam.** File to change:\n1507\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (§4.3): park-entry builders mark a\n1508\ttail-call park `:resume-mode :return-result`; `stack-restore` delivers\n1509\tsuch an entry by the return transition. Test to create:\n1510\t`test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`: a tail\n1511\teffectful call parked and resumed delivers through `K` (pops the frame,\n1512\ttruncates to its base, pushes the value) and halts with the value when\n1513\t`K` is empty; a tail call in operand position never executes the layout\n1514\tsyntax after it; non-tail parks are unchanged (the old stack tests stay\n1515\tgreen). **Depends on** nothing of 4b or 5.\n1516\t\n1517\t**6c proper.** Files to create: `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`\n1518\t(§4); `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc`; the two extra\n1519\tlanes added to the 6b harness file. **Depends on:** 5a, 5b (`derive`),\n1520\t6b, 6c-pre.\n1521\t\n1522\t**Acceptance (6c).**\n1523\t\n1524\t1. `layout-test/register-pairing`: for every `P ∈ C`, `phys` is total on\n1525\t   every body's named virtuals; `virt(p, s)` is single-valued at every\n1526\t   reachable boundary pc (§5.6 item 3).\n1527\t2. `layout-test/register-live-is-live-out`: §5.6 item 3's second clause,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc5fbdae-afe7-4b77-8da9-cb26d4729e39","uuid":"90c89b16-181c-4d56-bc57-6bc1685995b2","timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:19:21.287Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-6c/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\nexpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\nmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\nfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\nuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\nreason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n\n### 5.2 The matrix\n\n**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\nunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\nmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\npinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n`y = :arg`). **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), one exception to the\nmechanical rule (B2):** `nest`'s own lambda parameter is `x`, so under\n`:depth-3` any row whose program captures the free name `x` captures the\nwrapper's `x` instead. Row 21 is the one such row: its `(fn [] x)` is\ncreated inside the innermost `nest` application where `x = 1`, so the\nreference run itself answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]`, not `[3 [2 [1 99]]]`, and\nthe harness pins `1` for that one case (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc`,\nthe `(and (= 21 n) (= :depth-3 label))` branch). The bare, `:operand`\nand `:captured` results of row 21 keep `99`. The wrapper rule is\notherwise unchanged; the exception is stated rather than the wrapper\nrenamed, because the old harness's segments are shared and the lowered\nrun matches the reference either way (law 1 holds on the row). **Frames\nthe wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\ntrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\ndefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\nis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\nnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\noutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\nthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\nadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\nthe wrappers.\n\nPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\nof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\nreference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\non all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\njudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\nlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n\n| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 10 | install, blocked child (**amended after 6b**) | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, **phase `:parked`** | `'host.mod` | yes |\n| 11 | install, `:running` wire phase (**amended after 6b, synthetic phase**) | row 10's machine with the install entry's phase set to `:running` **by hand** before the lift | as 10 | act, `:install`, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | the program is row 10's; the phase is not engine-reachable at a safepoint (see below) |\n| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | yes; **amended after 6b:** the receiver composition is given the source's registry entry for `host.mod`, a composition prerequisite (§2.5): linked registry entries do not travel |\n| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes; **amended after 6b:** the fixture's module store is hand-built and keyed by the **symbol** `host.mod`, as `frame_test` does, while the kernel keys installed stores by module address; row 17 exercises a real installation |\n| 19 | halt | `(lit 42)` | — | result | `42` | yes |\n| 20 | **tail-effect halt** | run **outside the wrappers**, in two batches on one machine: a setup batch `(then (def! 's (stream/make 4)) (def! 'c (cursor-of s)))` run to halt (store writes persist across batches, as `lift_support.cljc:320-322` relies on), then the batch `(stream/next! c)` **marked `:tail? true` at the root**, `c` resolving through the store; no application precedes the call, so `K` is empty (any `let1` or wrapper would push a frame, §5.2 above) | append `\"B\"` | tail, `:next` | halts with `\"B\"` (4b: a tail effect with no frame to pop halts, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:205-211` stage 2; stack: the seam's empty-`K` case, §4.3), env free of `store-of` | yes |\n| 21 | **free-env shadowing** (the review's row) | initial free env `{x 99}`; `(let1 f (fn [] x) ((fn [x] (f (next-of c))) 1))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `E` at the read binds `x` to 1; root `free-env` carries `x 99` | the lowered `f` returns `99` (not nil, not 1) on every lane; **the one row the earlier design would have failed** | yes |\n| 22 | nil-valued live register | `(let1 pair (fn [p q] (cat (cat [] p) q)) (pair nil (next-of c)))`: the `nil` operand is evaluated **before** the read and is live across it (`(cat nil (next-of c))` would give `(conj nil \"B\")`, the list `(\"B\")`, so a vector-building `pair` is used; there is no `vector` primitive, `vm.cljc:380-410`, and `pair` builds through the segment's `cat`) | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `W` has a present key with `[:lit nil]` | `[nil \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 23 | tail FFI bookkeeping | a `tail` parked record under a call id `P ∈ :ffi-call-ids` | — | parked, context `:parked`, bookkeeping clause of P4 | validator accepts; the **same valid tail under a key not in `:ffi-call-ids`** is `:parked-site` (P4); moving `site` to a non-tail call is `:site` (T1, which runs first) | **synthetic**: no primitive returns an FFI effect through a tail `:call` today (evaluator §1.6) |\n| 24 | `act` FFI bookkeeping | 3's parked bookkeeping record under its call id | — | parked, P4 bookkeeping | validator accepts | 4b stage 2 |\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), rows 10-12 and the install contract\n(B1).** The earlier text promised a child \"`:blocked`, phase `:running`\"\n(row 10) and a child \"executing `:park`, kind `:parked`\" (row 11).\nNeither is an engine state at a quiescent safepoint. `advance-install`\n(`engine.cljc:1421-1454`) runs the child inside the sweep and then\nrecords it: a **blocked** child is retained at phase **`:parked`**\n(`:1444-1446`); a **halted** child goes straight to `link-install` in the\nsame sweep (`:1447-1452`), so it is never retained; and a child that\nexecutes `:park` is `:halted?` with a parked value, so it too goes to\n`link-install`, which completes the install if the exports are already\ndefined and otherwise refuses it `:export-missing`. Phase `:running` is\nassigned only in the `:else` arm, within one sweep, and is overwritten\nbefore any safepoint. So: row 10 is the engine's real state (child\n`:blocked`, phase `:parked`); row 11 exercises the `:running` **wire\nphase**, which the codec accepts (`install-node` admits `#{:running\n:parked}` on lift and `check-install!` on lower) and the harness\nconstructs by hand on row 10's machine (`installing % false :running`,\n`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:342`); row 12 is row 10's child under tail\ndelivery. No row promises an executable kind-`:parked` install child on\nthe current engine (owner question §11 Q1). **The install validation\nthe lower enforces** (`check-install!`, before any attachment, §7.2):\nthe phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\nretained response is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` map whose\n`:yin.module/name` equals the install's module name and whose\n`:yin.module/exports` is a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map\nwhose `:value` the receiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`);\nand the carried child task's kind is **`:blocked`** (`:install-child`),\nbecause a halted or parked child would have been linked in the sweep it\nhalted in. On lift, `install-node` refuses a phase outside the set as\n`:yin.k/non-portable :incomplete-install` and a response CBOR cannot\nencode as `:yin.k/non-portable :install-response`.\n\nRefusal rows: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`,\n`:591-603`); `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one\ninstruction past a `:const`, asked to lift); `:yin.k/non-portable\n:reason-mismatch` (the `:observe` hold, `:675-692`); and the validator's\n**membership tampers**, each a row-set edit of a valid body before\nlower: a window with an extra live key → `:window-extra`; a live key\nremoved → `:window-missing`; `rd` added → `:window-self`; `rd` replaced\nby another register → `:destination`; a return frame's `pc` moved after\na tail call → `:return-site`; a reason the site does not admit →\n`:reason-mismatch`; a `:tail` row given a fourth slot → **`:row-shape`\nat the codec's structure step (2), not `:frame-shape`** (amended after\n6b, B3: the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots, so the wire never\nreaches rule S1 with a fourth slot; S1 still governs an abstract frame\nhanded to `frame/defect` directly; and because `emit`/`emit-numbered`\nsilently drop extra slots from an abstract node, mapping over the\nshorter descriptor list, this tamper must be a **raw row edit with a\nrehash**, never an abstract-task edit);\nthe `[:lit nil]` of row 22 dropped → `:window-missing`; a `:reified`\nact whose `pc−1` is not a capture → `:captured-site`; a parked act whose\n`pc−1` is not `:park` → `:parked-site`; for E2, with row 21's initial\nfree environment extended to `{x 99 y 7}`, the **unshadowed** free\nbinding `y` in the act's `E` changed to `8` → `:free-env-mismatch`\n(changing the lexical `x`, which is `1` at the read, trips nothing: E2\ncompares off the lexical names), and the lexical `x` **removed** from\n`E` → `:free-env-mismatch` (presence clause (a)); the root `free-env`\nrow given a reserved key → `:reserved-name` (X1). **A changed live value**\n(e.g. `\"A\"` → `\"Z\"` in row 1's window) is not a membership defect and is\nnot expected to be refused: it is tested by **execution parity** (the\nlowered run's result differs from the reference), as the review ruled.\n\n### 5.3 Lanes\n\nThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\nengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\nand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\nthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\nthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n\n### 5.4 Laws\n\n1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n   `:368-401`).\n2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n   §5.5.\n3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n   reference result.\n6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n   verdict is lane-independent.\n\n### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n\n**PROPOSED** (reviewer: `stream-blind` rewrites decoded maps and does\nnot recompute Merkle ids, so it cannot establish root-id equality). The\nharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\ntheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\nlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\nlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\npair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\ntransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\n*host* traversal order** (the canonical walk of draft §6 is a function\nof the state and is what the procedure reads; the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\ntakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\nthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\nabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\nand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\ndoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\ncursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\nand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's body\nlabel (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as\namended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\nlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\nidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\ntheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\nthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\nenumeration order, so\na permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\nthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n\n### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n\n1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n   `:duplicate-param`.\n8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\n   values gives equal observables on rows 6, 9, 12, 20.\n9. Slices: for every non-tail safepoint with `m` return frames, the\n   `m+1` slices of §4.3 partition the stack kernel's operand stack\n   exactly, and each has the length of its `stack-layout`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n\n### 6.1 Code\n\n**DECIDED** (rulings 1, 3, 4; phase-5 §7.6, §9.2; v2 amendment §4's\n2026-10-10 note). The root's `code` slot is plain `{A canonical-vector}`\nof `\"v4\"` vectors, verified to hash to their key and to pass\n`code/well-formed?`. **No native image travels**: a stack or register\nreceiver derives `H(A)`/`R(A)` with `derive/stack-image`/`register-image`.\n`requires` carries UCF §7.6.1's map **plus** `:yin.k/trees {A #{tree-address …}}`,\none entry per address in `:yin.k/segments`; the **complete association\nrule** is phase-5's: an absent entry **or an empty set** is incomplete\n(`:yin.k/missing :yin.k/trees`, discovery `:incomplete`), and a claimed\naddress alone does not establish retrievability (a known address whose\nread fails now is the medium's `:absent`, not a missing association).\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), where the association comes from.**\nThe kernel holds no source index, so the tree association is a **lift\ninput**: `(rows/lift-task m serve! {:source-index {A #{tree-address …}}})`\nfrom the composition (the harness records `A → segment-key [:yin/tree\nast]` whenever it projects a vector). After the walk has fixed the\ncarried segments, the lift refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` (`:yin.k/discovery\n:incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/trees #{A …}}`) for any carried\nsegment whose entry is absent or empty; the lower repeats the same check\nover `requires`' `:yin.k/trees` against `code`'s keys\n(`check-dependencies!`). No source-index slot is persisted in the\ncarrier. *Why:* engineer deviation 1, accepted by codex: the index\nbelongs to the composition, not the machine.\n\n### 6.2 Rows, not datoms\n\n**DECIDED** (ruling 4). No vector is projected to datoms; the direct\nload path is the only path (§10.4).\n\n### 6.3 The free environment: carrier, source, ownership\n\n**DECIDED** by the review's finding and §4.4. The task's initial free\nenvironment travels **once, explicitly**, as the root's `free-env`\nslot, a `:bindings` row (`[S V …]` sorted). Every `E` in the task is a\ncomplete map and is checked by rules X1 and E2. On lower, the\nsemantic-register kernel receives it as the machine's base `:env`\n(`create-vm`'s `:env` option, `semantic_register.cljc:261`); a de Bruijn\nkernel receives it as its `:free-env` (`stack.cljc:418`) and\nreconstructs positional frames from each `E` by the chain (§4.4).\n\n**Where the lift gets it (PROPOSED, the gap the review named).** It is\n**never recovered from a current `E`** (§4.4). Its source is, per\nkernel:\n\n- **de Bruijn kernels**: the kernel's own `:free-env` field, \"fixed for\n  this instance\" (`stack.cljc:82`; `register.cljc:50`), read directly.\n- **the semantic-register kernel**: the evaluator keeps only the\n  mutable-current `:env` (`RegisterVM` field `env`, \"persistent lexical\n  scope map\", `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:23`; the old semantic\n  VM likewise captures the initial env only transiently in `eval`,\n  `semantic.cljc:874-876`), so no field holds the initial map. The\n  **composition owns it**: it is the value the composition passed as\n  `create-vm`'s `:env` (`vm/check-bindings!` of it, `:261`). The lift's\n  input contract therefore takes it explicitly,\n  `(rows/lift-task vm serve! {:free-env E₀})`, and refuses\n  `:yin.k/non-portable :missing-free-env` when the caller supplies none;\n  the holder and REPL paths that call the lift pass the environment they\n  constructed the task with. **Coordinated retention seam, recommended**:\n  4b (stage 3, its track's file) adds an immutable `free-env` field set\n  once in `create-vm` from the checked `:env` and never written by any\n  transition, with `gc-roots` listing it; once it lands the lift reads\n  it when `:free-env` is not supplied and the explicit argument stays as\n  the override for compositions that constructed the task elsewhere.\n  Phase 6 does **not** edit `semantic_register.cljc`; 6b depends on the\n  explicit argument and names the seam as a follow-up (§8.2).\n\n  **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the seam has landed and the lift reads\n  it.** The 4b track added the immutable `free-env` field\n  (`semantic_register.cljc:24`, set once in `create-vm` `:394`, a gc root\n  `:489`). `lift-task*` takes `(:free-env opts)` when supplied, else the\n  kernel's `(:free-env m)`, and refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :missing-free-env` only when neither exists. The explicit value\n  **overrides** the field without comparing against it; an override the\n  carried `E`s disagree with is caught by E2 when the lift validates its\n  own frames (`:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state`, e.g. `{x 5}`\n  against an `E` holding `x 99` off the lexical names). Install children\n  are lifted with the `:free-env` option removed, so a child always reads\n  its own kernel field.\n\nMerkle sharing means every `E` whose bindings equal another's shares one\n`:bindings` row; the root slot is one more reference to a row that\nusually already exists.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Lift and lower\n\n### 7.1 Lift\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): refuse unless quiescent; refuse the\nholds (`unliftable-hold`, `handoff.cljc:588`) as `:yin.k/non-portable\n:reason-mismatch`; classify kind; run the **dependency closure over the\n`\"v4\"` vectors**, which needs the phase-5 scanners\n(`scan/requirements`, `scan/free-names`, `scan/free-occurrences` over the\n§2.3 table: the current `completion/code-facts` reads stack-table\npositions and cannot be used), and refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied` unless\n`:complete`, `:yin.k/trees` included; number cursor cells (draft §6); walk\nwaits, parked records, the store slice, module stores, the free\nenvironment (from the kernel's `:free-env` field or the lift's explicit\n`:free-env` argument, §6.3, never from a current `E`), the result,\ninstall children; run `frame/defect` over every\nframe; emit rows and the census. A de Bruijn source walks the frames\n`layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail` reconstruct.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the order as `lift-task*` runs it (B4),\nand the discharge rule.** The paragraph above put the dependency closure\nbefore the numbering and the walk, but the carried segments, contexts\nand module stores are known only once the walk has found them, so the\nclosure cannot run first. The merged order is:\n\n1. **Quiescence and safepoint.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a\n   non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds (`unliftable-hold`: `:observe`,\n   `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`,\n   `:link-cursor-not-installed`) as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`;\n   refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set.\n2. **Kind.** `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked\n   table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted`\n   with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`.\n3. **Free environment** (§6.3): the option, else the kernel field, else\n   `:missing-free-env`.\n4. **The walk.** Waits (state and pending), the parked table, the whole\n   store, the result (halted only), install children (each a recursive\n   `lift-task*` in the child role with its own accumulator), then the\n   module stores by reachability. Every value is classified `[:lit x]`,\n   `[:row n]` or `[:cursor-cell source]`; references are authenticated\n   against the kernel's resource table before encoding\n   (`:forged-resource-reference`), a foreign-owned value is\n   `:foreign-value`, a heap cell `:cell`, a host object `:host-object`.\n   The walk records every **segment** reached and every **context**: a\n   closure's `(A, entry, E)`, an `act`'s `(A, pc, E)`, a return frame's\n   `(A, pc, E)`. A `tail` state contributes its `K` frames' contexts and\n   none for its own dead activation.\n5. **Code.** `{A vector}` for the segments reached, from the kernel's\n   code table through its aliases; an unaddressed segment is\n   `:yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment`, an address the kernel\n   cannot produce a vector for is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n6. **Dependency closure** (`discharge!`), **per context, over live\n   code.** For each recorded context `(A, at, E)`, the live pcs are\n   `(live-pcs v at)`: the closure of `at` under `analysis/successors`\n   plus, for every reachable `:closure` instruction, that closure's body\n   from its entry (reachable code can still construct it). Every free\n   occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) at a live pc must be **answered\n   in that context**, in `resolve-var`'s order: a reserved name; the\n   context's own `E`; the **one** store `engine/active-store` selects for\n   that `E` (the root store when `E` has no `store-of`, else the module\n   store it names, both of which travel); a profiled primitive (a\n   `:host`-class profile refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n   :host-state-primitive` at that point, so a host primitive shadowed by\n   `E` or the store lifts); an installing module's export (`m/x` built\n   with `(symbol (name m) (name x))`); a module the registry resolves for\n   a namespaced name. Any unanswered name is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:obligations #{…}}`.\n   *Why per context:* codex F3, the union of all module-store keys\n   answered names the running closure could not resolve. *Why live\n   code:* codex F6, a numeric or whole-body scan refused valid\n   continuations whose unreachable branches named a missing symbol.\n   The parser admits no backward jump (§3.1 of the frozen design), so no\n   carried vector exercises the backward edge; `live-pcs` is public and\n   is tested directly on a hand-built vector with a `:jump` to an earlier\n   pc. Discharge runs at lift only; the lower does not re-discharge free\n   names against the receiver (§7.2).\n7. **Trees** (§6.1): every carried segment has a non-empty entry in the\n   `:source-index` option, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees`.\n8. **The task.** `requires` is `{:yin.k/cursor-profiles … :yin.k/segments\n   … :yin.k/trees …}`; the root is assembled with `custody nil` (§2.5).\n9. **Frames.** `validate-frames` over the assembled abstract task (X1,\n   every wait, parked state and reified value, children recursively); a\n   defect is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` carrying it. The\n   lift does not decode its own output a second time.\n10. **Emit** (`emit`): canonical cursor-cell numbering and rows.\n\n**Refusals are thrown.** Every refusal of lift and lower is an `ex-info`\nwhose ex-data carries `:yin.k/status`, a `:yin.k/kind` where one is\nnamed, and the defect's data, the same convention as `decode` and `emit`;\nnothing returns a `{:status …}` map (engineer deviation 2, accepted).\n\n### 7.2 Lower\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §8 in order; §3.3): gate, structure, graph, hashes,\ncanonical form, canonical numbering, **frames**, pending and operations,\ndependency closure (`requires`/`code` agree with the census;\n`:yin.k/trees` complete per §6.1), custody, composition, restore. Restore\nbuilds the receiving kernel's state: semantic-register: `code` through\n`load-vector`/`attach-image` under `code/contract`, the free environment\nas base `:env`, each `:wait` to an engine entry (payload plus the\npending's engine fields, `lower-frame!`'s shapes, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`,\n`:deliver` carried), parked records into `:parked`, the result into\n`:value`; de Bruijn: derive and attach the image for each A, set\n`:free-env`, and `layout/lower-act`/`lower-tail` each frame (the stack\nlane's `tail` needs 6c-pre). Restoration delivers nothing, runs nothing,\nmints nothing.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the lower's staged checks as\n`lower-task` runs them, and its refusal kinds.** Steps 1-7 are `decode`\n(gate, structure, graph, hashes, canonical form, numbering, frames,\n§3.3). Steps 8-10 then run as four stages, **each over the whole task\ntree (root and every install child, pre-order) before the next**, and\n**every one of them before the first `attach!`**; a defect in a child's\nlater stage therefore cannot be masked by the root's earlier one, and\nzero streams are attached on any refusal (codex F1, F2, F4, F7; mutants\nthat attach early are killed by the attach-count law):\n\n| Stage | Function | Checks, in order | Refusal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| stores | `check-stores!` | no key of the root store or of any module store is a reserved name (`vm/reserved-name?`), refused here rather than by restore's `store-put` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name` (`:yin.k/role :store-key`) |\n| pendings and install records | `check-pendings!` | the task's role slot equals its position (`:root`/`:child`); every `:install` pending names a carried install row; kind consistency (`:halted` has no waits, `:blocked` has waits, `:parked` names a key of its parked table); then every install record by `check-install!` (§5.2: phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}`, the retained response and image contract, the child `:blocked`) | `:role`, `:incomplete-install`, `:kind`, `:parked-id`, `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`, all `:yin.k/undecodable` |\n| dependencies | `check-dependencies!` | `requires` is a map whose `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code` (`:requires`); every segment has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry; every `store-of` of every `:closure` and `:env` row of **this task** (children excluded, each checked in its own stage pass) names one of this task's `:module-store` rows (§2.6), so a parent's or sibling's store of the same key cannot answer (codex F7, F8); every vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`attaches?`: `module/attach-module` into a throwaway copy, the result discarded) (`:segment`); every `:closure` row names a `:closure` tuple of its vector at its entry (`:closure-origin`) with the same binders (`:closure-binders`); every `:prim` names a primitive the receiver holds | `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees` or **`:yin.k/stores`** (`:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`), `:yin.k/undecodable :segment`, `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/name p}` |\n| custody | `check-custody!` | the root's custody slot is nil (phase 6 carries fork only) | `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` |\n\nThen **attachment** (step 11, `attach-streams!`): one `attach!` per\ndistinct stream identity of the whole tree, `:yin.k/unsatisfied\n{:dao.stream/identity …}` when the composition declines. Then **restore**\n(step 12, `restore-task`): the carried vectors attached by A into a\nreceiver emptied of code, so frame and closure addresses map through the\nreceiver's own aliases and no source-local segment number travels; fresh\nresource keys per stream identity and per cursor cell; values re-sealed\nunder the receiver's owner; waits in `lower-frame!`'s entry shapes, a\n`tail` with `{:deliver :return}` and no window, env or pc; parked records\nwith a cycle guard; the store through `engine/store-put`; module stores;\ninstall children spawned by the engine's spawn discipline and then\nrestored recursively (the spawn's initial control is replaced, no\n`vm/run`); the free environment as both `:env` and `:free-env`; the id\ncounter `max(local, carried)`. Restore meets no carrier defect, by\nconstruction of the stages above. The lower does **not** re-discharge\nfree names against the receiver: a name a closure resolves through the\nregistry is the composition prerequisite of §2.5, and the receiver's\nregistry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n:kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n:install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\ncensus are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\nnamed none of them (deviation 9, accepted with the requirement that every\ndefect be a named transport outcome before attachment).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Slices, gates, entry conditions\n\nVerification follows `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: G0 per iteration on\nthe JVM, G1 `bb test:sub yin.vm` on three hosts, G2 `bb test:changed`,\nG3 on master.\n\n### 8.1 Slice 6a: the frame validator and the row codec (independent)\n\n**Files to create.**\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc` (§3): `defect`, public.\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (§2): the row table as data,\n  `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`, row ids (Jing default), the census and\n  numbering per the draft §6, `canonical-under` (§5.5, test-only but\n  placed here so three test namespaces share it). **Not** `lift-task`/\n  `lower-task`: full task transport is 6b.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame_test.cljc`,\n  `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`.\n\n**Depends on:** phase 3, 4a (landed), 4b stage 1 (branch) for live\nframes to validate. Touches nothing of 4b or 5; needs no scanner.\n\n**Acceptance (6a).**\n\n1. `frame-test/contract-is-pure`: `frame/defect` takes `[frame code\n   context task]`, `task` being plain data; the namespace's `ns` form\n   requires no kernel and no `yin.vm.engine` (a string check over the\n   source on the JVM lane, `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`).\n2. `frame-test/every-reference-frame-validates`: for every 4b-stage-1\n   row of §5.2 (1, 2, 5, 6, 13-16, 18, 19, 21, 22) over the four segments,\n   plus row 20 as one separate, unwrapped run (its empty `K` needs no wrapper\n   frames; §8.2 6b makes the same exception), every\n   `act`, `tail`, return frame, parked record and reified value the\n   register evaluator mints passes `defect` in its context with the\n   task context decoded from the machine's own tables.\n3. `frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper`: for each rule S1-S4, C1-C3,\n   A1-A6, T1, K1-K3, E1-E2, X1, P1-P4 one tampered frame built from a\n   valid one, refused with that rule's kind and a path naming the slot\n   (**twenty-four rules**: 4+3+6+1+3+2+1+4, the twenty-three of the\n   round-1 text plus X1; **twenty-five rows**, since C2 contributes two,\n   a float `pc` for `:pc-kind` and an out-of-range one for `:pc-bounds`;\n   X1's row goes through `frame/task-defect`); plus\n   `first-defect-is-structural`: a frame with a malformed `K` **and** a\n   bad window reports `:k-shape`.\n4. `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member`: row 22's frame\n   validates with the nil key present; the same frame with the key\n   removed is `:window-missing`.\n5. `frame-test/worked-example-saved`: on `:worked-example`, a frame at pc\n   4 with window `{1 <f>}` and `rd 2` validates; `{1 <f> 2 x}` is\n   `:window-self`; `{}` is `:window-missing`; `{1 <f> 3 x}` is\n   `:window-extra` (`saved(4, 2) = {1}`, `analysis_test.cljc:131-139`).\n6. `frame-test/free-env-mismatch`: row 21's frame (initial free\n   environment `{x 99 y 7}`) with `task :free-env {x 99 y 7}` validates;\n   with the free environment `{x 99 y 8}` or `{x 99}` it is\n   `:free-env-mismatch` (`y` is unshadowed); with the lexical `x` removed\n   from `E` it is `:free-env-mismatch` (presence); changing the lexical\n   `x` in `E` from `1` to `98` **does not** trip E2 (it is off the\n   comparison), and the test asserts that too; a `free-env` row with the\n   key `yin/def` is `:reserved-name` (X1).\n7. `rows-test/bijection`: `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame` and the converse\n   are identities on every frame of item 2, nil-valued keys included.\n8. `rows-test/row-ids-are-jing-default`: every content row's id equals\n   `(jing/segment-key body)` and names Jing's default algorithm; no\n   `sha256` appears in any id.\n9. `rows-test/window-sorted-and-unique`: an out-of-order or duplicated\n   `:window` row is `:yin.k/undecodable` at canonical form (step 5).\n10. `rows-test/canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`: §5.5's\n    transformation gives equal root ids for two emissions of one abstract\n    task under permuted cursor-cell numbering and distinct stream identities,\n    with the fixture's correspondence map; **and the unordered-container\n    permutation**: a store value that is a set of two stream references\n    and a map keyed by two cursor references, emitted twice with the two\n    streams' identities swapped (and the correspondence map swapped\n    accordingly), gives equal root ids; a marker absent from the map is\n    a test failure.\n11. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log\n    shows `Testing yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test` and\n    `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n    the maps of the numbering note's appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x\n    :y`, the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`,\n    the witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and\n    the two frame fixtures.\n13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…`; the path\n    pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned `d84480d4…`; each equal\n    after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…`; the witness numbers `B cc-1,\n    A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…`, and decodes; the balanced-alias witness\n    `#{[X X Y Y] [Z W Z W]}` gives one root for both enumerations, pinned\n    `5376f133…`, and the key without the local index would not.\n16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored, the two-2-cycle and\n    the 3-cycle-beside-fan states refuse at `emit` with\n    `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of\n    the numbering note's appendix A, the same verdict and path after\n    renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of each built with `emit-numbered`\n    decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the\n    path prefixed by the root, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a\n    parent whose install `'a` holds the 2-cycle refuses at `emit` with\n    path `[12 a 15]` and its `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the\n    `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n    `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (at least 2000\n    random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with install children)\n    in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its\n    root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and\n    census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict and path;\n    refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged against the\n    rewritten decoder; `numbering-precedes-frame-defect` is extended: the\n    2-cycle with the same reserved-name binding, carried by\n    `emit-numbered`, decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n    :unresolved-cursor-cells`, not `:reserved-name`.\n21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n    `[:depth]`, and so do one cell inside 3000 nested vectors under\n    `{:depth 3}` and under the default budget, and a literal nested 3000