{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fpj35hqahFi5W1dUuhCqAq","type":"tool_result","content":"1528\t   tail boundaries `[]`.\n1529\t3. `layout-test/register-rd-from-a`: on `:worked-example`'s register\n1530\t   lane at the inner call's park, the canonical `rd` is `2` while the\n1531\t   kernel's `dest` is `3`; the K frame of a nested non-tail call in\n1532\t   `:depth-3` has its `rd` from A, not its physical `dest`.\n1533\t4. `layout-test/stack-correspondence`: §5.6 item 4.\n1534\t5. `layout-test/stack-layout-is-the-saved-set`: §5.6 item 5 and the\n1535\t   worked cases: `(f (g x) y)` at the inner call → `[1]`; `(f (if c (g)\n1536\t   2) y)` at `(g)` → `[1]`.\n1537\t6. `layout-test/stack-slices-partition`: §5.6 item 9; and the\n1538\t   multi-frame fixture, the expression `(cat [1] ((fn [x] (cat [2] ((fn\n1539\t   [y] (cat [3] (next-of c))) 2))) 1))` under the harness's standard\n1540\t   binding setup: `with-cat` outermost, then `(let1 s (stream/make 4)\n1541\t   (let1 c (cursor-of s) …))` around it (the stream is made inside the\n1542\t   program, as every row does: that is this fixture's chosen setup. A\n1543\t   primitive `cat` supplied in the free environment would sit in a live\n1544\t   slot and is no carrier value, `:continuation-registers`,\n1545\t   `debruijn_register_effects.cljc:196`; an authentic cursor reference is\n1546\t   machine data with an explicit carrier marker, `engine.cljc:899-905`, so\n1547\t   in-program construction of `c` is a fixture choice, not a carrier\n1548\t   restriction). **Derivation of the expression\n1549\t   alone** (`cat` and `c` taken as given): the outer application of\n1550\t   `(fn [x] …)` pops its closure and `1`, leaving the outer `cat` and\n1551\t   `[1]` pushed, so its frame's base is `2`; the inner application leaves\n1552\t   the middle `cat` and `[2]` as well, base `4`; the current activation\n1553\t   has pushed its `cat` and `[3]` and the read has popped its cursor\n1554\t   operand (`stack.cljc:839`), so the operand stack has six values:\n1555\t   slices `[0,2)` = `[cat [1]]`, `[2,4)` = `[cat [2]]`, current `[4,6)` =\n1556\t   `[cat [3]]`, each slice's `stack-layout` being `[operator-rd\n1557\t   literal-rd]` of its `cat` call and equal to that activation's saved\n1558\t   window. **As run**, the three binding applications each pop their\n1559\t   closure and single operand off an otherwise empty stack, so `K` has\n1560\t   **five** frames with bases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0)\n1561\t   [0,2) [2,4)`, current `[4,6)`, stack length six; the test pins these\n1562\t   bases from the kernel's state, checks the partition, and checks that\n1563\t   lowering the lifted frame emits the same five bases (§4.3; the\n1564\t   before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`).\n1565\t7. `layout-test/env-reconstruction`: §5.6 item 6.\n1566\t8. `layout-test/closures-round-trip`: §5.6 item 7.\n1567\t9. `layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable`: §5.6 item 8.\n1568\t10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every row ×\n1569\t    segment under `canonical-under`.\n1570\t11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs.\n1571\t12. `harness/layout-refusals`: `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel\n1572\t    stepped to a non-safepoint pc; a register entry whose `live` was\n1573\t    tampered reconstructs a frame the validator refuses (`:window-extra`\n1574\t    or `:window-missing` by the tamper's direction).\n1575\t13. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts.\n1576\t\n1577\t### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n1578\t\n1579\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 7). 6a-6c green on three hosts; the row\n1580\tcarrier the only writer and reader the semantic-register kernel has; a\n1581\tdocumented seam for the holder, checkpoint, ledger and authority paths\n1582\t(phase 7 wires it; phase 6 edits none of them); the Stage D/E scenarios\n1583\t(`stage_e_scenarios.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`) listed with, for\n